<GuideLayout title="Intermediate 1.4 · Farming, Desert Treasure & Earth Orbs" current="intermediate">
  <div class="page-grid">
    <aside class="sidebar"><IntermediateNav current="1-4" /></aside>
    <article class="content">
      <header class="chapter-header">
        <div class="chapter-kicker">Intermediate · Chapter 1.4</div>
        <h1 class="chapter-title">Convert the early foundation into a real midgame account.</h1>
        <p class="chapter-summary">This subsection has four connected payoffs: protection prayers, a passive Farming foundation that leads into contracts when eligible, Ancient Magicks, and a large Earth-orb stock for the Chapter 2 battlestaff cash loop. The quest order below is deliberately linear, with exact future items called out only when they save a meaningful return trip.</p>
      </header>

      <ChapterGoals
        summary="You are finishing the quest/stat web prepared in 1.3 and deliberately preparing the first several Chapter 2 blocks. The major breakpoints are 43 Prayer, 18 Slayer into Animal Magnetism, 45 Ranged, a passive Farming routine, 46 Hunter, Desert Treasure, 108 Paddewwa tablets and 1,590 charged Earth orbs. Claim the Chapter 1 Crafting quest rewards before choosing a mushroom-pie boost or a measured permanent-level bridge. Also bank the small carryovers Chapter 2 expects: a spare bucket of sap, a harralander, 2,000 stardust and—if birdhouse RNG cooperates—two growing teak trees."
        stats={["70 Woodcutting — 75 only if you still lack 13 magic logs", "43 Prayer · 45 Ranged · 20+ Slayer · 46 Hunter", "40 Herblore · 42 Smithing · live Crafting check for the battlestaff start", "Farming routine started; live level, not a fixed Chapter 1 exit", "63 Magic · live 53 Thieving check for Desert Treasure", "39 Construction target for the Chapter 2 handoff", "Optional AFK detour: 65 Attack / 65 Strength / 65 Defence"]}
        unlocks={["Earth-battlestaff start: actual 58 Crafting or enough mushroom-pie boosts", "The Grand Tree + Eyes of Glouphrie", "Garden of Tranquillity + Eadgar’s Ruse", "Passive Farming; easy contracts when eligible", "My Arm’s Big Adventure", "Ava’s attractor via Animal Magnetism", "The Hand in the Sand / Bert's daily sand", "Ancient Magicks via Desert Treasure", "108 Paddewwa tablets", "1,590 charged Earth orbs"]}
      />

      <div class="callout"><strong>Full Chapter 1 finish line:</strong> roughly 46 Attack · 50 Strength · 34 Defence · 39 Hitpoints · 45 Ranged · 43 Prayer · 63 Magic · 24 Runecraft · 39 Construction · actual 50+ Agility · 40 Herblore · <strong>live Thieving (at least 53 for Desert Treasure), full Rogue and the 1.2 liquid-cash stop</strong> · <strong>live Crafting level sufficient to start Earth battlestaves with or without a pie</strong> · roughly 47–48 Fletching (46 is the deliberate checkpoint) · 20+ Slayer · 46 Hunter · 44 Mining · 42 Smithing · actual 65+ Fishing · 60 Cooking · 89 Firemaking · 70 Woodcutting · <strong>Farming routine started, with its exit level determined by your real runs and seeds</strong>. Fishing may be higher if ordinary Barbarian Fishing supplied the relaxed Agility remainder; neither 74 Fishing nor 77 Thieving is a required checkpoint. Farming 49 is checked at Fairytale II in 2.6 and actual 70 before Song of the Elves.</div>

<TrainingOptions id="i14-woodcutting-training" informational={true} skill="Woodcutting" target="actual 70; caps are optional" why="Actual 70 Woodcutting is a later Song of the Elves gate. Sulliusceps overlap with this grind and may supply mushroom-pie caps, but Chapter 1 has not yet claimed Animal Magnetism, Enlightened Journey or The Hand in the Sand Crafting XP. Do not decide to grind permanent 58 Crafting—or stay solely for caps—before those rewards land." recommended="Train normally to 65, then use Sulliusceps for some or all of 65→70 if you enjoy the loop. Bank up to five caps if they arrive reasonably, but stop at actual 70 Woodcutting even if they do not. Decide on pies only at the final live Crafting check." relaxed="Forestry, ordinary teaks or another comfortable method to actual 70 are fully valid. No Sulliuscep cap is a required outcome; a later live Crafting bridge can replace the boost." resource="Before Desert Treasure, bank 13 magic logs: twelve for this quest and one for Mourning's End Part I. Wintertodt may already have supplied them. If you reach 70 and still have fewer than 13 banked, continue to 75 and cut the missing logs yourself. The later MM2 balloon logs come from While Guthix Sleeps' actual storeroom loot, so they are not another three-log requirement here. If you did not have 60 Woodcutting during the Zeah block in 1.3, buy the rune and mithril axes at the Woodcutting Guild when you reach it." fast="2-tick teaks are optional and never assumed." />

      <RouteStep id="i14-sulliuscep-caps" number="1" title="Reach actual 70 Woodcutting; bank Sulliuscep caps only if convenient">
        <p><strong>If you have never done Fossil Island or Sulliusceps before, do not treat this as “just go chop mushrooms.”</strong> Sulliusceps are giant mushrooms inside the <strong>Tar Swamp on Fossil Island</strong>. Only one of six locations is active for your character at a time; after you chop the active mushroom down, the next one becomes active and you continue around the route. The first trip is the confusing one. Once the southern teleport is unlocked and you have seen the loop once, repeated clears are much simpler.</p>
        <p><strong>Bring:</strong> your <strong>rune axe</strong> (or dragon axe if Wintertodt gave you one), a <strong>rake</strong>, <strong>antipoison</strong>, plenty of <strong>food</strong>, Fossil Island access such as a <strong>digsite pendant</strong>, and a teleport out. Keep several free inventory spaces for caps, fossils and other drops. The swamp has dangerous tar monsters and poisonous spine mushrooms; chopping is fairly low-attention, but moving between spots is not walk-away AFK.</p>
        <ol>
          <li><strong>Get into the swamp:</strong> use the Fossil Island mushtree network to reach <strong>Mushroom Meadow</strong>, then head south into the Tar Swamp. Cut blocking vines/roots with your axe and <strong>rake the ground vines</strong> when the route requires it.</li>
          <li><strong>Unlock Sticky Swamp immediately:</strong> on the first lap, activate the <strong>Sticky Swamp Magic Mushtree</strong> near the southern/bottom part of the route. From then on you can teleport straight back to the useful end of the swamp instead of repeating the ugly entrance every trip.</li>
          <li><strong>Avoid the hazards instead of fighting them:</strong> do not intentionally fight tar monsters. Keep moving, eat when needed, and use terrain to get away. Antipoison is for the spine-mushroom poison.</li>
          <li><strong>Follow the rotation:</strong> find the active Sulliuscep, chop it until it depletes, then move to the next active location. After your first complete lap, this becomes a repeatable circuit rather than a scavenger hunt.</li>
          <li><strong>Bank caps if they arrive while training to 70.</strong> Five caps can make four mushroom pies later, but they are not a condition for finishing this step. Birdhouse runs do not award Sulliuscep caps.</li>
        </ol>
        <p><strong>Stop at actual 70 Woodcutting.</strong> A five-cap hunt may take roughly 45–75 minutes once familiar, but its random tail is not part of required progress. If five caps arrived, bank them. If not, continue the quests; their Crafting rewards may reduce or eliminate any later bridge. The required checkbox here is Woodcutting 70 only.</p>
        <p><a href="https://oldschool.runescape.wiki/w/Sulliuscep/Strategies" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Open the OSRS Wiki Sulliuscep strategy</a> before the first run and keep its swamp-route image handy. Keep useful unidentified fossils you receive; they can be identified for Varrock Museum displays/Kudos later.</p>
      </RouteStep>

      <div class="later-note"><strong>Hold the Crafting decision:</strong> keep any caps banked. Complete the coming quests and their Crafting rewards first; the final Chapter 1 checkpoint explains the pie recipe or a live-XP permanent bridge only if needed.</div>

      <RouteStep id="i14-gnomes" number="2" title="Complete The Grand Tree, then The Eyes of Glouphrie">
        <p>Complete <strong>The Grand Tree</strong> from start to finish. Then complete <strong>The Eyes of Glouphrie</strong>. These are now one clean Gnome Stronghold quest block rather than quests you started hours ago and periodically revisited.</p>
        <p>After Eyes of Glouphrie, enchant the <strong>crystal saw seed</strong>. Keep the crystal saw; it becomes a useful Construction booster later.</p>
        <p>Before leaving this block, bring a <strong>knife + 1 empty bucket</strong> to an <strong>evergreen tree</strong> (the trees between Barbarian Village and Falador are convenient). Use the knife on the tree with the bucket in your inventory and bank <strong>1 bucket of sap</strong>. Early Chapter 2 needs it for <strong>Icthlarin's Little Helper</strong>; collecting it now prevents an otherwise pointless return trip.</p>
        <p><strong>Before leaving the bank:</strong> check the quest-specific quantities and sources in the preparation note.</p>
        <ExpandableNote label="Quest preparation" title="Grand Tree and Eyes of Glouphrie supplies">
          <p><strong>The Grand Tree:</strong> carry 1,000 coins for the Stronghold entry if Femi's earlier help was skipped, plus combat gear, food and a teleport. The translation book, bark, keys and Daconia rock are quest-local; bank combat equipment only when a quest leg actually calls for it.</p>
          <p><strong>The Eyes of Glouphrie:</strong> <strong>1 bucket of sap, 1 mud rune, 1 maple log, 1 oak log, hammer, saw and pestle and mortar</strong>. Use the mud rune deliberately bought at MTA; if lost, return to the MTA rewards shop for a replacement, checking its point price before spending reward points. Cut the two logs at 45/15 Woodcutting if the banked ones were fletched. For sap, use a knife on an evergreen with an empty bucket. Make <strong>two buckets of sap total</strong>: this quest consumes one and Icthlarin needs the other. Buy a saw at the Varrock Sawmill if missing. The coloured discs and crystal-saw seed come from the quest.</p>
        </ExpandableNote>
      </RouteStep>

      <RouteStep id="i14-garden" number="3" title="Complete Garden of Tranquillity and bank the compost with a purpose">
        <p>You already have at least <strong>33 Farming</strong> from the Chapter 1.3 quest rewards and Creature of Fenkenstrain is complete, so do <strong>Garden of Tranquillity</strong> from start to finish now. Bring the <strong>ring of charos</strong> from 1.3.</p>
        <div class="later-note"><strong>Farming math:</strong> By this point the route has awarded at least <strong>19,500 Farming XP</strong>: 1,000 from the Goblin generals subquest, 3,500 from Fairytale I, 5,000 from Forgettable Tale, and 10,000 from The Garden of Death. That is at least <strong>level 33</strong>. Garden of Tranquillity adds another <strong>5,000 XP</strong>, so both <strong>Enlightened Journey (30 Farming)</strong> and <strong>My Arm's Big Adventure (29 Farming)</strong> are already covered with no hidden farm run.</div>
        <p><strong>Make the compost rather than assuming it appeared:</strong> fill an ordinary compost bin with <strong>15 weeds or other compostable produce</strong>, close it and let it process for roughly an hour while you continue the quest. Garden of Tranquillity rewards a four-dose compost potion. Once the bin is ready, open it and use <strong>one dose on the full bin</strong> to upgrade its 15 normal compost to <strong>15 supercompost</strong>; collect it with 15 empty buckets. Bank those 15 before leaving this Farming block:</p>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>8 supercompost are reserved for My Arm’s Big Adventure: seven for the soil and one for the crop.</strong></li>
          <li>The remaining <strong>7</strong> are a small Farming/Herblore buffer; use these as needed while keeping the eight My Arm buckets reserved.</li>
        </ul>
        <p>Other compost-potion doses can upgrade later normal compost; volcanic ash from Fossil Island can later turn supercompost into ultracompost.</p>
        <div class="later-note"><strong>Start the Chapter 2 teak timer now:</strong> Garden of Tranquillity pushes the guaranteed quest XP past <strong>35 Farming</strong>. If you have the <strong>2 teak seeds</strong> reserved from birdhouses, put each seed in a <strong>filled plant pot</strong> with a gardening trowel, water it, wait for the sapling, then plant the two saplings in the <strong>two western Fossil Island hardwood patches</strong> with a spade. Use <strong>supercompost/ultracompost</strong> on the patches. Teaks take roughly three days to grow, and early Chapter 2 can use two mature player-grown trees for the plank grind. If birdhouse RNG has given you only one or zero seeds, plant what you have and continue birdhouse/eligible Farming Contract runs; <strong>Chapter 2.1 has a Castle Wars teak fallback</strong>, so neither chapter waits on the missing seed or growth timer.</div>
        <p><strong>Start ordinary tree and fruit-tree saplings now too, if seeds are banked.</strong> Open the <a href="#i14-farming-training">combined Farming circuit below</a> now if you need patch directions. At the next convenient Catherby visit, plant any banked <strong>limpwurt seed</strong> in its flower patch as well: Temple of Ikov will consume <strong>20 unnoted roots</strong>, so giving this short crop an early timer helps. Return to quest 4 and check mature crops at later logins. This is not a 62-Farming stop sign. Keep any western Fossil Island timber teaks standing after their first health check until Chapter 2.1 has its logs.</p>
        <p><strong>Before leaving the bank:</strong> check the quest-specific quantities and sources in the preparation note.</p>
        <ExpandableNote label="Quest preparation" title="Garden of Tranquillity's actual seeds and tools">
          <p>Bring the ring of charos, rake, seed dibber, spade, secateurs, watering can, gardening trowel, <strong>2 plant cures, 1 marigold seed, 3 cabbage seeds, 3 onion seeds</strong> (six of each allotment seed gives a replant buffer), a fishing rod or fly fishing rod, hammer, <strong>1 rune/pure essence, pestle and mortar, 1 filled plant pot and 2 buckets of compost</strong>.</p>
          <p>Sarah's farm shop south of Falador sells the tools, plant pots, compost and plant cure. Olivia in Draynor sells the marigold/cabbage/onion seeds; the quest's special rose/orchid/delphinium/snowdrop seeds are obtained from the gardeners. Buy a rod from Gerrant if the Fishing rod is missing and mine one essence through Aubury's teleport. Fill the plant pot from a farming patch with the trowel. Plant and tend the actual quest crops, keeping the requested mature marigolds in place until the gardener's dialogue accepts them; earlier random seed rewards are not the supplier.</p>
        </ExpandableNote>
      </RouteStep>

      <RouteStep id="i14-eadgar" number="4" title="Complete Eadgar’s Ruse and take the two future goutweed now">
        <p>You reached <strong>31 Herblore</strong> in 1.3, so complete <strong>Eadgar’s Ruse</strong> in one pass with no boost required. The quest awards <strong>11,000 Herblore XP</strong>.</p>
        <p>When you reach the goutweed storeroom, collect <strong>3 goutweed total</strong> before leaving. One is used to finish Eadgar’s Ruse; bank the <strong>2 extras</strong> for <strong>Dream Mentor</strong> and <strong>Dragon Slayer II</strong>. This is a worthwhile future pickup because returning to the storeroom is genuinely annoying.</p>
        <p><strong>Before leaving the bank:</strong> check the quest-specific quantities and sources in the preparation note.</p>
        <ExpandableNote label="Quest preparation" title="Troll truth serum, the parrot and the ranarr you must reserve">
          <p>Bring climbing boots, <strong>1 vodka, pineapple chunks, 2 normal logs, 10 grain, 5 raw chicken, tinderbox, pestle and mortar and 1 ranarr potion (unf)</strong>. Heckel Funch in the Grand Tree sells vodka/pineapple; cut the pineapple into chunks with a knife. Pick ten grain from a wheat field and kill five Lumbridge chickens, keeping the meat raw. Cut the two logs separately if none remain.</p>
          <p>At 30 Herblore, put <strong>one clean ranarr in a vial of water</strong> and keep it unfinished for the quest's troll-thistle mixture; a prayer potion does not substitute. If no ranarr remains, kill Taverley Dungeon chaos druids for the missing herb using the <a href={`${base}intermediate/chapter-1-3/#i13-quest-supplies`}>early material suppliers</a>. This ranarr is spent before DT's prayer-potion bank check. The parrot, dirty robes and troll thistle are collected during the quest.</p>
        </ExpandableNote>
      </RouteStep>

      <RouteStep id="i14-kourend-easy" number="5" title="Finish Kourend & Kebos Easy and put the 2,500 XP lamp on Herblore">
        <p><strong>Do not grind Herblore to 40.</strong> BruhSailer reaches 40 through guaranteed quest/diary XP plus incidental potion XP. Because this guide only guarantees the exact <strong>31 Herblore</strong> needed for Eadgar’s Ruse, we make the diary lamp explicit so the breakpoint is deterministic.</p>
        <div class="callout"><strong>Guaranteed Herblore math from the minimum route:</strong> from the actual-31 floor of 14,833 XP, add <strong>Eadgar’s Ruse</strong> 11,000, this diary lamp 2,500, and <strong>My Arm’s Big Adventure</strong> 10,000: at least <strong>38,333 Herblore XP</strong>, above the <strong>37,224 XP</strong> needed for level 40. These rewards are counted once. Jungle Potion's 775 XP is not needed for this floor, so the calculation also works if Fairytale I made you complete it before the level-31 check; extra herbs/lamps remain bonus progress.</div>
        <p>Complete any Easy tasks you have not already done. Bring a <strong>pickaxe, weapon, spade, 8,750 gp, tarromin potion (unf), 1 limpwurt root, fly fishing rod and feathers</strong>. If you do not already have a limpwurt root, kill <strong>Hill Giants</strong> until one drops.</p>
        <ol>
          <li>Mine <strong>iron ore at the Mount Karuulm mine</strong>.</li>
          <li>Kill a <strong>Sand Crab</strong>.</li>
          <li>Hand in a <strong>book at the Arceuus Library</strong>.</li>
          <li>Steal from a <strong>Hosidius fruit stall</strong>.</li>
          <li>Browse the <strong>Warrens General Store</strong>.</li>
          <li>Take the boat to <strong>Land’s End</strong>.</li>
          <li>Pray at the altar on the <strong>top floor of Kourend Castle</strong>.</li>
          <li>Dig up <strong>saltpetre</strong> south-west of Hosidius.</li>
          <li>Speak to an <strong>Estate Agent</strong>, move your POH to <strong>Hosidius for 8,750 gp</strong>, then enter it through the Hosidius portal. Leave the house there for now.</li>
          <li>Complete a lap of either <strong>Shayzien Agility Course</strong>.</li>
          <li>In the <strong>Lovakengj pub</strong>, add the limpwurt root to the tarromin potion (unf) to make a <strong>strength potion</strong>.</li>
          <li>Fish a <strong>trout from the River Molch</strong> south-east of the Farming Guild.</li>
        </ol>
        <p>Claim <strong>Rada’s blessing 1</strong> and the <strong>2,500 XP antique lamp</strong> from Elise at Kourend Castle. Put the lamp on <strong>Herblore</strong>.</p>
        <p>The strength potion spent <strong>one</strong> limpwurt root. Keep that root separate from the <strong>20 unnoted roots</strong> Temple of Ikov will later consume. Kourend &amp; Kebos Easy now also protects the Hosidius flower patch from disease; plant a banked limpwurt seed there when you next pass the farming plots, then harvest/replant through the combined circuit below until the Ikov stock is ready.</p>
        <div class="later-note"><strong>Current-game note:</strong> Kourend Favour was removed in 2024. If an old guide tells you to gain Hosidius/Shayzien favour for these tasks, ignore it; the current Easy diary uses the tasks listed above.</div>
        <p><strong>Before leaving the bank:</strong> check the quest-specific quantities and sources in the preparation note.</p>
        <ExpandableNote label="Quest preparation" title="Make the diary potion and recover the rod/root">
          <p>A tarromin potion (unf) is <strong>one clean tarromin added to a vial of water</strong>; the diary then adds <strong>one limpwurt root in the Lovakengj pub</strong> at 12 Herblore. The two tarromins previously used for Shades of Mort'ton are already spent. Use a banked herb or hunt one at Taverley chaos druids; for the root, bring a brass key from the Edgeville Dungeon hill-giant area and fight the hill giants via the hut west of Varrock, or use the accessible Hosidius peninsula hobgoblins. Stop after one actual root, keeping the later Ikov stock separate. Buy the fly fishing rod and feathers from Gerrant in Port Sarim if the Fishing kit is missing.</p>
        </ExpandableNote>
      </RouteStep>

      <RouteStep id="i14-myarm" number="6" title="Complete My Arm’s Big Adventure and let the quest finish 40 Herblore">
        <p>This is the payoff for the setup already completed: <strong>Eadgar’s Ruse</strong> is done, <strong>The Feud</strong> and <strong>Jungle Potion</strong> are complete, 1.3 reached at least <strong>60% Tai Bwo Wannai favour</strong>, and the Farming quest rewards already cover the <strong>29 Farming</strong> requirement.</p>
        <p>Bring <strong>3 buckets of Ugthanki dung</strong>, <strong>8 supercompost</strong>, climbing boots or Trollheim teleport access, some coins, and normal combat gear/food. A bucket can be obtained during the quest. The Trollheim tool leprechaun can supply a rake, seed dibber, spade and plant cure when needed.</p>
        <p>Complete <strong>My Arm’s Big Adventure</strong> from start to finish. During the Tai Bwo Wannai section, Murcaily gives you the separate <strong>hardy gout tuber</strong>; this is not the regular gout tuber banked during Cleanup in 1.3.</p>
        <p>The quest awards <strong>10,000 Herblore XP</strong> and <strong>5,000 Farming XP</strong>. Following the minimum XP route above, this is the point where <strong>40 Herblore is guaranteed</strong>. No extra herb-processing grind is required.</p>
        <div class="later-note"><strong>Favour and tuber are separate:</strong> My Arm spends 60 percentage points of Tai Bwo Wannai favour and supplies its own <em>hardy</em> tuber. A <em>regular</em> gout tuber, if you chose the optional Karamja Medium preparation, is not consumed here; bank it for that diary. Starting at the required 60% leaves 0%; starting at optional 100% leaves 40%.</div>
        <p><strong>Before leaving the bank:</strong> check the quest-specific quantities and sources in the preparation note.</p>
        <ExpandableNote label="Quest preparation" title="Obtain the dung and finish the crop-supply check">
          <p><strong>Ugthanki dung:</strong> take a spade, three empty buckets and travel money to Pollnivneach. Ask Ali/Isma'il the Kebab Seller for red hot sauce, put it in the trough at the camel seller's enclosure, wait for the camel to produce dung and use the buckets on it. You do not need a Camulet or Enakhra's Lament for this method.</p>
          <p><strong>Supercompost:</strong> seven buckets fertilise the soil; reserve an <strong>eighth for the later crop</strong> and buy a plant cure from the Trollheim tool leprechaun for disease during the cutscene. Seven plus plant cure can work, but the eight-bucket preparation is easy from the fifteen-bucket Garden reward batch. If that batch was used, put 15 charter-shop pineapples in a compost bin, close it, return after rotting and bucket the supercompost. Keep the eight quest buckets as supercompost rather than converting the whole batch to ultracompost.</p>
          <p>Retrieve/buy rake, seed dibber and spade from the Trollheim tool leprechaun; the seed dibber is still needed after Barbarian Farming training. Bring an empty bucket for the opening goutweedy-lump step. Rebank before the Baby Roc/Giant Roc fight for Magic gear, food and a teleport. This occurs before the route's 43-Prayer step: use the parasol/rock flinch in the current quest guide if prayers are not unlocked, or take an early 43-Prayer detour explicitly before relying on Protect from Missiles.</p>
        </ExpandableNote>
      </RouteStep>

      <RouteStep id="i14-smith" number="7" title="Reach 42 Smithing and finish the Chapter 1 jewelry stock">
        <p>Use Blast Furnace or a normal Smithing method to reach <strong>42 Smithing</strong>. Then bring your teleport jewelry up to the full Chapter 1 target. <strong>Check actual Crafting before the ruby amulet:</strong> it requires 50 Crafting, so finish the mandatory glass-orb blowing checkpoint first if still below 50; do not count an unmade amulet as banked.</p>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>40 rings of dueling</strong></li>
          <li><strong>45 digsite pendants</strong></li>
          <li><strong>10 games necklaces</strong></li>
          <li><strong>8 bracelets of clay</strong></li>
          <li><strong>2 sapphire amulets</strong> — turn one into an <strong>amulet of magic</strong></li>
          <li><strong>1 ruby amulet</strong> — enchant it into an <strong>amulet of strength</strong></li>
          <li><strong>1 binding necklace</strong></li>
        </ul>
        <p>Count what you made in 1.3 and only craft the difference. These are a <strong>convenience stock target</strong>, not evidence that random Wintertodt/Tempoross rewards contained 107 gold bars and all the gems. If short, use 1.3's extra Shooting Star gem-bag route, the East Ardougne gem merchant's small stocked supply, and Ordan's gold ore; stop when the <em>remaining</em> jewelry is made, not after a fixed number of bags/worlds. Preserve the 2,000 stardust earmarked for the Chapter 2 Celestial ring. Do not delay a quest over the five optional jade passage necklaces; Shilo Village gem rocks in 2.2 can supply them later.</p>
      </RouteStep>

      <RouteStep id="i14-family" number="8" title="Complete Family Crest">
        <p>Complete <strong>Family Crest</strong> as a standalone quest. Keep the resulting gauntlet access; the quest is also an important prerequisite for later account progression.</p>
        <p><strong>Before leaving the bank:</strong> check the quest-specific quantities and sources in the preparation note.</p>
        <ExpandableNote label="Quest preparation" title="All five cooked fish, perfect jewellery and four blast spells">
          <p>Bring <strong>one cooked shrimp, salmon, tuna, bass and swordfish</strong>, pickaxe, <strong>two cut rubies, ring mould, necklace mould</strong>, at least one dose of ordinary antipoison and runes for <strong>all four elemental Blast spells</strong>. The fifth fish is bass, not cod. Do not consume the later Myreque salmon reserve.</p>
          <p>At Catherby, use a small net for shrimp, a big net for bass and a harpoon for tuna/swordfish (46/50 Fishing cover the latter methods); fly-fish salmon at Barbarian Village. Buy the nets/harpoon from Harry and rod/feathers from Gerrant, then cook until one of each remains unburnt. Buy rubies from the Ardougne gem merchant if the jewellery batch used them all, and moulds from Dommik. Mine the quest's <strong>perfect gold ore</strong> and smelt it at 40 Smithing; ordinary gold bars are not substitutes.</p>
          <p>For Chronozon, successfully hit with <strong>Wind, Water, Earth and Fire Blast</strong>. Actual 59 Magic is required to cast Fire Blast without a boost; if below 59, train the live gap with ordinary combat, or bring Wizard's mind bombs for a +3 boost from at least 56 and refresh it before the required cast. Start with <strong>20 death, 65 air, 20 water, 20 earth and 25 fire runes</strong> to allow repeat casts after misses (a matching staff reduces element use). Aubury/Betty sell these; buy a small refill if needed. Buy antipoison from Jiminua rather than assuming an unmade potion. Bring food and a teleport for the dungeon fights.</p>
          <p>After completion, choose <strong>chaos gauntlets from Johnathon</strong> if you want the bolt-spell damage bonus for DT. If the gauntlets were already enchanted differently, check the conversion cost before switching; the first family-gauntlet reward is not automatically the chaos version.</p>
        </ExpandableNote>
      </RouteStep>

      <RouteStep id="i14-prayer" number="9" title="Bank about 75–80 dragon bones with a green → blue dragon hybrid, then reach 43 Prayer at the Chaos Altar">
        <p><strong>Dragonfire-protection check:</strong> before equipping the anti-dragon shield for this block, start <strong>Dragon Slayer I</strong> with the Guildmaster in the Champions' Guild (32 Quest Points are already secured), ask about protection, and collect the shield from Duke Horacio upstairs in Lumbridge Castle. Current mechanics require the quest to be <em>started</em> to equip the shield. This is a deliberate partial unlock for safe dragon gathering; finish the whole quest in 2.2.</p>
        <p><strong>The dragon bones are the Prayer method, but you do not need to spend the entire grind standing in the Wilderness.</strong> Green dragons are much faster and easier to kill, so start there while the area is quiet. If PK traffic becomes annoying—or if you simply do not want to risk another trip—finish the bone stack safely on <strong>Isle of Souls blue dragons</strong>. The bones are identical for Prayer purposes, so the two methods mix perfectly.</p>

        <p><strong>Target:</strong> aim for roughly <strong>75–80 dragon bones total</strong> before using the Chaos Altar. The exact number varies because earlier Prayer XP differs and the altar's 50% bone-save effect is random. If you already have Prayer XP above the route baseline, you may finish with fewer; stop immediately once you reach <strong>43 Prayer</strong>.</p>

        <h3>Part A — fast first batch: green dragons near Corporeal Beast</h3>
        <p><strong>Bring:</strong> a <strong>games necklace</strong>, <strong>water staff</strong>, <strong>air runes</strong>, <strong>chaos runes</strong>, <strong>mind runes</strong>, <strong>anti-dragon shield</strong>, <strong>amulet of magic</strong>, a <strong>looting bag</strong>, and cheap food such as the banked salmon. Wear only gear you are comfortable losing and leave your cash stack in the bank.</p>
        <ol>
          <li>Use the games necklace to teleport to <strong>Corporeal Beast</strong>.</li>
          <li>Leave the cave into the Wilderness and run <strong>west</strong> to the nearby green dragons. Green dragons have a <strong>50% weakness to Water spells</strong>, which is why Water Bolt works so well here.</li>
          <li>Use <strong>Water Bolt</strong> while you have spare chaos runes. <strong>Preserve your final 100 chaos runes</strong> for later Chapter 2 needs; once you approach that reserve, switch to <strong>Water Strike</strong>.</li>
          <li>Pick up <strong>every dragon bone and every green dragonhide</strong>. Put hides in the looting bag first and keep bones in the inventory. Bank early rather than carrying a stack you would hate to lose.</li>
          <li><strong>Do not force the entire 75–80-bone grind here.</strong> Roughly <strong>30–40 green-dragon bones</strong> is a good fast first batch, but the real rule is simple: stay while it feels quiet and efficient; leave as soon as PK activity turns the method into a nuisance.</li>
        </ol>

        <h3>Part B — safe finish: Isle of Souls blue dragons</h3>
        <p><strong>If Isle of Souls is new to you:</strong> this is a normal non-Wilderness island containing Soul Wars and a small dungeon. You do <strong>not</strong> need to play Soul Wars or complete its tutorial just to use the island. The easiest route is the <strong>Soul Wars minigame teleport</strong>, which lands you at Nomad's camp on the Isle of Souls. There is a bank in the Soul Wars lobby, making it a convenient place to resupply between dragon trips. <a href="https://oldschool.runescape.wiki/w/Isle_of_Souls_Dungeon" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Open the OSRS Wiki Isle of Souls Dungeon page</a> if you want the map beside you on the first run.</p>
        <ol>
          <li><strong>Getting there:</strong> open the <strong>Grouping / Minigames</strong> interface and use the <strong>Soul Wars</strong> minigame teleport. If that is unavailable or on cooldown, fairy ring <strong>BJP</strong> lands on the northern coast of the Isle of Souls; the <strong>Soul Wars portal in Edgeville</strong> is another fallback. You do not need to enter a Soul Wars match.</li>
          <li>From the Soul Wars lobby, run <strong>north-east across the island</strong> to the dungeon entrance on the <strong>eastern side</strong> of the Isle of Souls and climb down.</li>
          <li><strong>Dungeon route:</strong> follow the dungeon around past the demon room and the fire-giant room. The <strong>third main room</strong> contains <strong>four adult blue dragons and three baby blue dragons</strong>.</li>
          <li><strong>Safespot:</strong> the easiest landmark is the small <strong>cove containing two adamantite rocks</strong> on the eastern side of the blue-dragon room. Walk inside the stalagmites and cast from there; the adult dragons cannot reach you. Keep the <strong>anti-dragon shield equipped</strong> while moving through the dungeon.</li>
          <li><strong>Magic:</strong> blue dragons also have a <strong>50% Water weakness</strong>. Water Bolt is faster if you have spare chaos runes, but <strong>Water Strike is completely acceptable</strong> here and lets you preserve the 100-chaos-rune Chapter 2 reserve. With the safespot established, this is much lower-stress than the Wilderness method.</li>
          <li>Bank <strong>every dragon bone</strong> and, if you have inventory space, the <strong>blue dragonhides</strong> as well. A looting bag does not help outside the Wilderness, so simply bank at the Soul Wars lobby and run back. Continue until your combined green + blue dragon bones reach roughly <strong>75–80 total</strong>.</li>
        </ol>

        <div class="later-note"><strong>Keep one spare dragon bone banked after reaching 43 Prayer.</strong> Do not blindly empty the bank just because bones remain.</div>

        <h3>Part C — cash the bones in at the correct Wilderness Chaos Altar</h3>
        <div class="callout"><strong>There are two different Wilderness locations called the Chaos Temple. You want the small <em>Chaos Temple (hut)</em> in level 38 Wilderness, northwest of the Forgotten Cemetery and west of the Lava Maze.</strong> Do <strong>not</strong> use the level ~11 Chaos Temple near Ferox that is packed with Zombie Pirates and aggressive level-129 Elder Chaos Druids. Those druids can hit hard, repeatedly Tele Block you and pull you back toward them. If you see a crowd of druids/pirates, you are at the wrong temple. <a href="https://oldschool.runescape.wiki/w/Wilderness" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Open the OSRS Wiki Wilderness map</a> and look for the level-38 <strong>Chaos Temple (hut)</strong>.</div>
        <p><strong>Default route:</strong> bank all combat gear at <strong>Ferox Enclave</strong>, take only <strong>one disposable inventory of dragon bones</strong>, then run north into deep Wilderness toward the <strong>Forgotten Cemetery</strong>. Continue <strong>northwest</strong> to the small level-38 temple just south of the Frozen Waste Plateau. If you already have a <strong>burning amulet</strong>, its <strong>Lava Maze</strong> teleport is a faster optional route; run west/southwest from the maze to the same level-38 hut.</p>
        <p>Inside the correct hut, use the bones on the <strong>Chaos Altar</strong> until the inventory is gone, then return and repeat. The altar gives the same <strong>350% XP</strong> as a gilded altar with both burners lit, and every offered bone has a <strong>50% chance not to be consumed</strong>, so a single bone can be offered multiple times. The hut itself is a known PK hotspot, which is why this guide deliberately risks only one bone inventory at a time.</p>

        <ExpandableNote label="Optional Wilderness tips" title="Give yourself the best chance to hop before a PKer lands the first hit">
          <p><strong>Before the first trip, set Private chat to Friends or Off.</strong> With Private set to Friends, players who are not on your Friends List see you as offline. That prevents a random PKer from simply adding your name and using private-chat status to follow your world between trips.</p>
          <p><strong>Keep the logout/world-switch interface ready while you offer bones.</strong> A very practical setup is to open the World Switcher and leave the cursor over a different world. If another player logs in beside the altar and has not hit you yet, switch immediately instead of waiting to decide whether they look dangerous. Once you are in combat, you cannot world-hop and must be out of combat for 10 seconds first.</p>
          <p><strong>Do not always hop to the next world in the list.</strong> Choosing a world several entries away is a reasonable anti-pattern habit because PKers often cycle through worlds looking for altar users. This is not protection or a guarantee—just a small way to make your next destination less predictable.</p>
          <p><strong>The first hit is the important one.</strong> There is very little you can do once a geared PKer has landed a freeze/Tele Block and put you in combat. Your best defence on this low-risk route is noticing the login quickly, hopping before the first attack, and never risking more than one inventory of bones.</p>
          <p><strong>Wine of Zamorak is for a clean suicide after the inventory is finished.</strong> When no player has damaged you, repeatedly taking the wine is a convenient way to die and return to your respawn point instead of running south. <strong>Do not rely on the wine to deny a PKer your remaining bones.</strong> If another player has already damaged you recently, the death uses Wilderness PvP death rules even if the wine deals the final point of damage; lost tradeable items are awarded under the PvP rules, generally to the player who dealt the most damage. If a PKer appears before touching you, hopping is the better escape attempt.</p>
        </ExpandableNote>

        <ExpandableNote label="Optional safety note" title="If you die and your bones are sitting in a dangerous gravestone">
          <p><strong>You usually do not need to keep death-running a dangerous PvM grave.</strong> A normal gravestone lasts for 15 minutes of logged-in time, and you can ask <strong>Death</strong> to collect its contents for you. Ferox Enclave has access to <strong>Death's Office</strong>, so if retrieving the grave itself is becoming a second death trap, bank anything you kept and go there instead.</p>
          <p><strong>The confusing “some items have been moved to Death's Office” message:</strong> if you die again while an existing grave already contains lots of normal unstackable items, the grave has a capacity rule. Excess items from the existing grave can be moved to Death's Office before the new death is added. That can make it look as though only the three items you kept on death survived, even though the rest may simply have been transferred to Death.</p>
          <p>For this route, ordinary dragon bones are individually worth less than 100,000 coins, so reclaiming those low-value items from Death is normally <strong>free</strong>. Valuable equipment can have reclamation fees, which is another reason the altar trips should contain essentially nothing except disposable bones.</p>
          <p><strong>Important:</strong> this explanation is for an ordinary gravestone/PvM death. A death involving another player in the Wilderness can use different item-loss rules. Always check the <strong>Items Kept on Death</strong> screen before risking anything you care about.</p>
        </ExpandableNote>

        <p><strong>Stop the moment you reach 43 Prayer.</strong> Any unused bones stay banked for later.</p>
        <div class="optional-efficiency"><strong>Why this hybrid is recommended:</strong> green dragons save time while the Wilderness is quiet; Isle of Souls removes the PK risk when it is not. The Chaos Altar still supplies the enormous Prayer-efficiency gain, but you only risk one small bone inventory at a time instead of your entire gathering grind.</div>
      </RouteStep>

      <RouteStep id="i14-imp" number="10" title="Complete Imp Catcher before finishing the Ranged target">
        <p>Check your bank for the four beads: <strong>red, yellow, black and white</strong>. If any are missing, kill imps around the <strong>Tower of Life / Ardougne area</strong> until you have one of each, then complete <strong>Imp Catcher</strong>.</p>
        <p>This is placed here because the Magic XP is still useful and it closes another Chapter 1 quest without creating a special trip later.</p>
      </RouteStep>

      <RouteStep id="i14-slayer-ranged-30" number="11" title="Use one Slayer assignment to reach 18 Slayer, and train Ranged to 30">
        <p><strong>Check finished steel arrows independently of Fletching XP.</strong> A player already at 46 Fletching may have made none; a player just below it may have made only a few batches. Count the bank, set aside <strong>75 steel arrows</strong> for Ava's accumulator upgrade at 50 Ranged, and start with roughly <strong>500 additional usable arrows</strong>. Buy only the shortfall as finished arrows from <strong>Lowe's Archery Emporium in eastern Varrock</strong>; the shop stocks them, so take manageable 250–500-arrow batches and return if the usable stack runs low. Do not buy 1,200 throwing knives or keep Fletching past 46 merely to fund this Ranged training. Shadow of the Storm already supplied <strong>10,000 Ranged XP</strong>, so the baseline account is around 27 Ranged and only needs a short push to 30.</p>
        <p>The Slayer gap is also intentionally small. By this point the guide has guaranteed <strong>3,000 Slayer XP</strong>: 1,000 from the Varrock Museum Natural History Quiz, 1,000 from A Porcine of Interest, and 1,000 from Recipe for Disaster: Freeing the Mountain Dwarf. That puts the baseline account at <strong>16 Slayer</strong>; level 18 is only <strong>523 XP</strong> farther away.</p>
        <ol>
          <li><strong>If you already have an active Slayer task, use it.</strong> Otherwise go to <strong>Vannaka in Edgeville Dungeon</strong> and ask for an assignment. Vannaka requires 40 Combat, which the normal route has already cleared. Receiving his task also completes the corresponding <strong>Varrock Medium Diary</strong> task for later.</li>
          <li>Before leaving Vannaka, check whether the assignment needs a Slayer item such as earmuffs, a mirror shield or a bag of salt and buy it there if necessary.</li>
          <li>Use your <strong>best shortbow + live steel-arrow stack</strong> on the assignment whenever the monster/location is reasonable to range. A willow shortbow is already sufficient for the steel arrows; a maple shortbow at 30 is a small optional upgrade, not another required shopping stop.</li>
          <li><strong>Reach 18 Slayer.</strong> If the assignment is awkward to range, kill the monsters with melee/magic instead; the important requirement is 18 Slayer, not forcing every Slayer kill to double as Ranged training.</li>
          <li>If you are still below <strong>30 Ranged</strong> when 18 Slayer is reached, finish the short gap at <strong>Gemstone Crab</strong>, Sand Crabs or another comfortable safespot using the same steel arrows.</li>
        </ol>
        <div class="callout"><strong>Why Vannaka instead of moving another quest forward?</strong> The only easy quest XP that belongs here has already been collected: the Museum quiz, A Porcine of Interest and the Mountain Dwarf subquest are the reason the account is already 16 Slayer. The next large quest reward, Wanted!, would require dragging Black Knights' Fortress and Recruitment Drive forward too. One small Slayer assignment is cleaner.</div>
        <p><a href="https://oldschool.runescape.wiki/w/Vannaka" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Open the OSRS Wiki Vannaka page</a> if you need the Edgeville Dungeon map or assignment details.</p>
      </RouteStep>

      <RouteStep id="i14-animal-magnetism" number="12" title="Complete Ernest the Chicken, then Animal Magnetism for Ava’s attractor">
        <p>Now that you have <strong>18 Slayer and 30 Ranged</strong>, pull Animal Magnetism forward instead of waiting until Chapter 2. The other requirements are already covered: <strong>The Restless Ghost</strong> and <strong>Priest in Peril</strong> are complete, and the route is far above 19 Crafting / 35 Woodcutting.</p>
        <p><strong>First complete Ernest the Chicken</strong> in Draynor Manor. The quest does not require outside supplies; collect the pressure gauge, rubber tube and oil can inside/around the manor, solve the basement lever puzzle, and return to Professor Oddenstein. Quest Helper is fine for the lever puzzle.</p>
        <p><strong>Then prepare Animal Magnetism.</strong> Bring or obtain:</p>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>1 mithril axe</strong> — the one deliberately bought at the Woodcutting Guild in 1.3 / the 60 Woodcutting checkpoint. No other axe substitutes for this quest step.</li>
          <li><strong>5 unnoted iron bars</strong>, <strong>hammer</strong> and your <strong>ghostspeak amulet</strong>.</li>
          <li><strong>20 ecto-tokens.</strong> If you do not already have them, take 4 bones, 4 empty pots and 4 buckets to the Ectofuntus north of Port Phasmatys. Grind the four bones upstairs into bonemeal, fill the four buckets from the slime pool downstairs, worship four times, then talk to a ghost disciple while wearing the ghostspeak amulet. Each worship is worth 5 tokens.</li>
          <li><strong>1 hard leather.</strong> If missing, tan a cowhide into hard leather at Ellis in Al Kharid.</li>
          <li><strong>1 holy symbol.</strong> If missing, use the separate conditional silver bar reserved in 1.1: make an unblessed symbol with a holy mould, string it with wool, then have Brother Jered upstairs in the Edgeville Monastery bless it. Your 43 Prayer already clears the monastery's 31 Prayer entry requirement. If the bar was spent, refill it at the East Ardougne Silver Merchant first; do not borrow Desert Treasure's bar.</li>
          <li><strong>Polished buttons.</strong> Check the bank first; if missing, pickpocket H.A.M. members in the hideout east of Draynor until you get buttons, then polish them by clicking them.</li>
        </ul>
        <p>Complete <strong>Animal Magnetism</strong> from start to finish. At under 50 Ranged, Ava rewards <strong>Ava’s attractor</strong>. Wear it for the rest of the Ranged grind: it automatically recovers <strong>60% of fired ammunition</strong>, another 20% drops to the floor, and 20% breaks. Do not wear a metal torso that interferes with the device.</p>
        <div class="later-note"><strong>Reserve at least 75 steel arrows.</strong> At 50 Ranged, Ava can upgrade the attractor to <strong>Ava’s accumulator</strong> using 75 steel arrows; the accumulator automatically recovers 72% of fired ammunition.</div>
        <p><a href="https://oldschool.runescape.wiki/w/Animal_Magnetism" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Open the OSRS Wiki Animal Magnetism guide</a> if you want the dialogue/puzzle walkthrough while following this route's preparation list.</p>
        <p><strong>Before leaving the bank:</strong> check the quest-specific quantities and sources in the preparation note.</p>
        <ExpandableNote label="Quest preparation" title="Recover the bars, holy mould and everyday consumables">
          <p>For five missing iron bars, mine or buy iron ore and smelt at 15 Smithing until <strong>five successful bars</strong> are banked; normal smelting can fail, so five ore is not a guarantee. Buy a hammer, empty pots and buckets from a general store; ordinary chicken bones work for the four ecto-token worships. Buy the holy mould from Dommik and one ball of wool from Aemad in East Ardougne, or shear sheep north of Lumbridge and spin the wool at the castle's first-floor wheel. These materials are additional to earlier consumed silver and wool. If the ghostspeak amulet is lost, ask Father Urhney in Lumbridge Swamp for another. If the mithril axe was missed, buy exactly that tier at the Woodcutting Guild now that 60 Woodcutting is covered.</p>
        </ExpandableNote>
      </RouteStep>

      <TrainingOptions id="i14-ranged-training" skill="Ranged" target="45" why="Shadow of the Storm gets you into the high 20s, the Vannaka/18-Slayer checkpoint gets you to 30, and Animal Magnetism gives Ava's attractor before the longer AFK portion. Finish 45 here so Temple of Ikov and the remaining quest combat are comfortable; the separate 46-Fletching XP checkpoint does not guarantee an arrow quantity." recommended="Equip Ava's attractor, your best Ranged gear, a shortbow and the usable steel-arrow stack checked in Step 11, then use Gemstone Crab in the Tlati Rainforest. Children of the Sun was completed in 1.2, so Varlamore is already unlocked. Travel to Varlamore, run south into the Tlati Rainforest, and enter any of the three Gemstone Crab mine caves; the caves route you to whichever mine currently has the crab. The crab effectively has infinite HP and stays in place for about 10 minutes; when it burrows, use the nearby cave to follow it. It gives 12.5% less XP per hit than normal targets, but the long idle windows and lack of overkill downtime make it excellent for this guide's AFK preference." relaxed="Sand Crabs or Ammonite Crabs are equally valid low-attention alternatives if you prefer familiar crab spots. With Ava's attractor equipped, most arrows return automatically; occasionally pick up the floor pile if you care about stretching the stack even farther." resource="Check the live usable-arrow stack during the 30-to-45 grind; buy another manageable batch of finished steel arrows from Lowe in eastern Varrock only when low. Keep the separate 75-arrow Ava's accumulator reserve banked. Loar Shades remain valid if you specifically want shade remains, but Chapter 1 does not require them." fast="Throwing knives remain a faster 2-tick alternative if you want higher-input Ranged XP/hour. They are not the default; this route uses affordable, refillable steel arrows and prioritizes low-attention training." />

      <RouteStep id="i14-optional-melee" number="Optional" optional={true} title="Optional AFK break: take melee to 65 Attack / 65 Strength / 65 Defence at Gemstone Crab">
        <p>If you enjoy nearly second-screen combat, stay at <strong>Gemstone Crab</strong> after 45 Ranged and train melee to <strong>65 Attack, 65 Strength and 65 Defence</strong>.</p>
        <p>Equip the <strong>rune sword</strong> purchased in 1.3, your <strong>amulet of strength</strong>, and the best melee armour already in the bank. Attack the crab, leave auto-retaliate on, and move through the nearby cave when the crab relocates roughly every ten minutes. Train the three melee stats in whatever order you prefer.</p>
        <p><strong>Why 65/65/65?</strong> 65 Attack + 65 Strength is exactly the <strong>130 combined</strong> needed to enter the Warriors' Guild. 65 Defence already passes the <strong>60 Defence</strong> needed to equip a dragon defender and meets the <strong>actual, unboosted 65 Defence</strong> gate for King's Ransom. <strong>70 Defence</strong> for Piety still comes later.</p>
        <div class="optional-efficiency"><strong>This is genuinely optional.</strong> The mandatory Chapter 1 finish line remains about 46 Attack / 50 Strength / 34 Defence. If you skip this AFK detour, Chapter 2 will explicitly train the guild, defender and King's Ransom gates when they matter; it will never assume you took this branch.</div>
      </RouteStep>

      <RouteStep id="i14-journey" number="13" title="Complete Enlightened Journey">
        <p>Complete <strong>Enlightened Journey</strong> for balloon transport and its <strong>2,000 Crafting XP</strong>. You are already guaranteed <strong>30 Farming</strong> from the earlier quest XP math, so there is no extra Farming grind. Go directly to The Hand in the Sand next; A Soul’s Bane follows that quest.</p>
        <p><strong>Before leaving the bank:</strong> check the quest-specific quantities and sources in the preparation note.</p>
        <ExpandableNote label="Quest preparation" title="Balloon materials and the willow-branch timer">
          <p>Bring <strong>3 papyrus, 1 ball of wool, 1 full sack of potatoes (10), 8 additional empty sacks, 1 unlit candle, yellow dye, red dye, 10 silk, an empty bowl, 10 normal logs, tinderbox and 12 willow branches</strong>. Buy papyrus and wool from Aemad in East Ardougne, sacks from Sarah's farm shop, and an unlit candle from Catherby's candle shop. Pick ten potatoes in the Lumbridge field and fill one sack; the other eight must stay empty. Steal silk from the Ardougne stall or buy it from the Al Kharid silk trader.</p>
          <p>For dyes, give Aggie in Draynor <strong>2 redberries + 5 coins</strong> for red and <strong>2 onions + 5 coins</strong> for yellow; Wydin sells redberries and onions grow north of Rimmington. A bowl is available at a general store. Cut the ten ordinary logs; willow logs do not replace the ordinary fuel in this opening flight.</p>
          <p><strong>Branches are not logs.</strong> Auguste supplies a willow seed: use a filled plant pot/trowel/watering can to make its sapling, plant it in a tree patch at 30 Farming and pay the gardener a <strong>basket of five apples</strong> for protection if desired. Buy the basket from Sarah and apples from a food shop. Once the willow is grown, use secateurs to cut branches, returning for regrowth until you hold twelve; keep the tree standing while doing this. A regular wild willow cannot supply the branches. Other balloon destinations require separate logs later; this quest does not consume the Grand Tree's three-magic-log unlock.</p>
        </ExpandableNote>
      </RouteStep>

      <RouteStep id="i14-hand" number="14" title="Complete The Hand in the Sand immediately after Enlightened Journey">
        <p><strong>Read actual Crafting first:</strong> this quest needs <strong>actual 49 Crafting</strong> and 17 Thieving to start, with no prerequisite quest or mandatory fight. Thieving is already covered. If below 49, blow only enough of the protected 1,590-orb batch to reach 49; the orbs require actual 46, already checked in 1.3. You may use banked gems or separately sourced glass for a shortfall, but do not consume quest glass. At 49, blow <strong>one lantern lens</strong> from the glass reserved for this quest.</p>
        <p><strong>Bank check before Bert in Yanille:</strong> bring about <strong>150 coins</strong>, <strong>one empty vial</strong> (a second makes Zavistic's Port Sarim teleport convenient), <strong>one white berry</strong>, <strong>one lantern lens</strong>, <strong>five earth runes</strong>, and <strong>one empty bucket</strong> or a bucket of sand. If missing, buy a vial from a Herblore shop, a bucket from a general store and the earth runes from Betty in Port Sarim for 20 coins. Bring one beer and redberries if already banked; otherwise buy the beer at Yanille's Dragon Inn and redberries at Port Sarim's food store during the quest. The quest supplies its own sand from Sandy in Brimhaven; fill the bucket at Yanille's sandpit for the separate final bucket-of-sand step.</p>
        <p><strong>White-berry fallback now, before Farming Contracts:</strong> check the bank first. If none are banked, bring food and kill level-37 <strong>chaos druid warriors</strong> in the <strong>Yanille Agility Dungeon</strong> until one drops; the first balancing ledge needs 40 Agility, already covered. This is a repeatable one-item drop hunt (listed at 5/128 per kill), not a reason to force 59 Farming or visit Wilderness. Do not use the dungeon chaos altar: it drops you into poison spiders. A berry already grown/banked is fine.</p>
        <p>Start with <strong>Bert by the Yanille sandpit</strong>, then ring the bell <strong>outside</strong> the Wizards' Guild to call Zavistic Rarve. You do <strong>not</strong> need the normal guild-entry Magic level for this quest. Follow Bert's/Sandy's rota and Betty's truth-serum sequence, visit Entrana briefly, and finish the quest for <strong>9,000 Crafting XP</strong>, 1,000 Thieving XP and Bert's daily sand option. The <a href="https://oldschool.runescape.wiki/w/The_Hand_in_the_Sand/Quick_guide" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">current quick guide</a> covers dialogue if needed.</p>
      </RouteStep>

      <RouteStep id="i14-glass" number="15" title="Complete A Soul’s Bane and finish the 1,590 unpowered orbs">
        <p>Complete <strong>A Soul’s Bane</strong> now; it was deliberately placed after Enlightened Journey → The Hand in the Sand. This is the deadline for the orb stock, <em>not</em> the time to decide on permanent 58 Crafting.</p>
        <p>Part 1.3 already established the full glass plan, and this is the deadline. Your original <strong>1,600 molten glass</strong> should be accounted for as:</p>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>1,590 unpowered orbs</strong></li>
          <li><strong>6 molten glass</strong> reserved for <strong>Desert Treasure</strong></li>
          <li><strong>1 molten glass</strong> already blown into the <strong>lantern lens</strong> for <strong>The Hand in the Sand</strong></li>
          <li><strong>1 molten glass used for the birdhouse crafting table 2</strong> in 1.3, plus <strong>up to 2 spare molten glass</strong>; one may have become Pirate Pete's fishbowl, leaving one for Chapter 2</li>
        </ul>
        <p>If you split the bankstanding grind across the chapter, <strong>finish every remaining unpowered orb now</strong>. The 1,590 orbs give <strong>83,475 Crafting XP</strong>. At an actual-46 floor established <em>after</em> smelting, the orbs alone end at <strong>151,458 XP</strong>, not 183,458: the earlier 32,000 smelting XP is already inside that level-46 floor. Subsequent guaranteed quest XP, the lens and any jewellery raise the live total. <strong>Do not train to 58 here:</strong> the final handoff checks the actual level after the remaining quests.</p>
        <p><strong>Before leaving the bank:</strong> check the quest-specific quantities and sources in the preparation note.</p>
        <ExpandableNote label="Quest preparation" title="A Soul's Bane needs a rope and a separate combat inventory">
          <p>Take <strong>one rope</strong> from Ned in Draynor, food, a teleport and combat gear before entering the rift. The anger weapons, fear/confusion rooms and other special objects are supplied inside the quest. Bring normal combat supplies for the final fight; you do not need to buy or craft the anger weapons beforehand.</p>
        </ExpandableNote>
      </RouteStep>

      <RouteStep id="i14-quests" number="16" title="Complete Biohazard and Ardougne Easy, then Watchtower">
        <p>Complete Biohazard, the short diary, then Watchtower; rebank between activities:</p>
        <ol>
          <li><strong>Biohazard</strong></li>
          <li><strong>Ardougne Easy Diary</strong></li>
          <li><strong>Watchtower</strong></li>
        </ol>
<p><strong>After Biohazard, finish Ardougne Easy before leaving the area.</strong> Rune Mysteries and the 5-Thieving gate are already covered. Open the <a href="https://oldschool.runescape.wiki/w/Ardougne_Diary#Easy" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Easy task list</a> and check each entry: Cromperty's essence teleport, East Ardougne cake and silk stalls (sell a silk for 60 coins), the church altar, one <em>started</em> Fishing Trawler trip (the outfit is not required for this task), Biohazard's Combat Training Camp, Tindel Marchant's rusty-sword identification, the Ardougne Wilderness lever, Aleck's Yanille shop and Probita's pet check. Pickpocket H.A.M. members for a rusty sword if none is banked; Tindel charges 100 coins and a rare junk result requires another sword. For the lever, <strong>bank valuables first</strong>, pull it into Wilderness, then return immediately. Claim <strong>Ardougne cloak 1</strong>, its unlimited monastery teleport and the 2,500-XP lamp (use it on Herblore; later conservative minimums do not depend on it); its other useful payoff is <strong>200 instead of 100 death runes</strong> when a grown cat is traded to a West Ardougne civilian. This diary has a named rune/travel use, not a generic efficiency checkbox.</p>
        <div class="later-note"><strong>Optional background cat loop:</strong> after the diary, let a kitten follow during suitable long sessions, feed/stroke it as needed (stackable raw karambwanji become convenient after Tai Bwo Wannai Trio), then trade an <em>extra</em> grown cat in West Ardougne for 200 death runes. Growth takes roughly three hours <em>following</em>, not three hours in the bank. Do not promise a fixed number of cats/runes. <strong>Reserve one grown, non-overgrown cat for RFD Evil Dave in 2.4</strong> before trading extras; the follower slot can be left free whenever an activity needs it.</div>
        <p>My Arm’s Big Adventure has deliberately been moved earlier in this page so the <strong>40 Herblore breakpoint is resolved immediately after Eadgar’s Ruse and the Kourend Easy lamp</strong>, rather than being hidden inside an unrelated multi-quest block.</p>
        <p><strong>Before leaving the bank:</strong> check the quest-specific quantities and sources in the preparation note.</p>
        <ExpandableNote label="Quest preparation" title="Biohazard and Watchtower kits after the diary detour">
          <p><strong>Biohazard:</strong> bring the gas mask; replace it from the cupboard in Edmond's house if missing. The bird feed, pigeon cage, samples and other special items are collected through the quest. Carry each sample in the form the quest gives it and rebank for the diary afterwards.</p>
          <p><strong>Watchtower:</strong> bring <strong>20 coins, 1 gold bar, 1 death rune, pickaxe, 1 dragon bones, 2 ropes, 1 guam potion (unf), lit candle/lantern, pestle and mortar, 1 bat bones and 1 jangerberries</strong>. Use the dragon bone specifically retained after 43 Prayer; if spent, kill one more blue dragon using the earlier shield/safespot setup. Smith one gold ore at 40 Smithing (buy ore from Ordan), buy the rune from Aubury/Betty and ropes from Ned, and kill a bat by Keep Le Faye for the bat bones. A clean guam plus a water-filled vial makes the unfinished potion at 3 Herblore; use Taverley chaos druids if none remains.</p>
          <p>For jangerberries, use a banked Hosidius fruit-stall berry or use one of the two ropes to reach the ogre island west of Yanille and pick its ground spawn. The second rope remains for the later cave entry. The potion's other special ingredients and the skavid map/crystals come from quest steps; follow the mixing order before giving the mixture to the Watchtower wizard. Keep food and a teleport for Gorad and the ogre caves.</p>
        </ExpandableNote>
      </RouteStep>

      <TrainingOptions id="i14-farming-training" informational={true} skill="Farming" target="start the passive Farming circuit" why="Actual 62 opens boosted medium Farming Contracts and 65 removes the boost, but neither is the next quest's hard gate. Keep doing the guide while crops grow; check actual 49 at Fairytale II and actual 70 before Song of the Elves." recommended="Follow the combined trees, hops and herbs circuit below at a convenient login: check mature crops, replant from banked seeds, then go play. The run takes attention; growing happens passively even while logged out." relaxed="Do fewer accessible patches when seeds are scarce or crops are still growing. Return at a later session; no alarm, second daily circuit or full-patch quota." resource="Birdhouses may yield seed nests; easy contracts give random seed packs. Hops can protect scarce mahoganies, herbs support potions, occasional hardwood checks give XP, and giant seaweed mainly supports later Crafting." fast="Tithe Farm is an optional timed, higher-input bridge for a chosen level or reward. You never need to complete its shop to progress." />

      <p><strong>Contract milestones, not a Chapter 1 deadline:</strong> the Farming Guild's beginner wing and Jane's easy contracts need <strong>45 Farming</strong>; a Garden pie can reach that from <strong>actual 42</strong>. At <strong>actual 62</strong>, the same +3 boost reaches the <strong>65</strong> intermediate wing and medium contracts. At <strong>actual 65</strong>, mediums need no pie. Reach the Guild via fairy ring <strong>CIR</strong> with your dramen staff, then walk south-west. You must be boosted when asking Jane for <em>each</em> higher-tier contract, including “another contract”; existing easy contracts cannot be cancelled, whereas medium/hard contracts can be downgraded. Start with attainable easy assignments using banked potato, onion, cabbage or flower seeds rather than waiting for a high-level preplant layout. A completed assignment gives a <strong>random seed pack</strong>, never a chosen tree seed. Once the intermediate wing is available, add its ordinary tree patch to Guild visits; its fruit-tree patch is in the <strong>85-Farming</strong> wing, not this early circuit. <a href="https://oldschool.runescape.wiki/w/Farming_contract" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Contract rules</a>.</p>

      <div class="callout"><strong>Garden pie supply for passive players too:</strong> a Garden pie gives <strong>+3 Farming</strong>. The <strong>Cooks’ Guild west of Varrock</strong> requires 32 Cooking and a chef's hat; this route already exceeds the Cooking level. Use a banked hat or buy one from <strong>Shayzien Styles</strong>. Romily Weaklax's Pie Shop inside has a base stock of <strong>two Garden pies at 24 coins each</strong> and restocks; hop or return later only if you actually need more. Refresh the boost before each boosted Guild entry/contract request. If you choose Tithe at actual 51–53, a pie can also unlock Bologano seeds at effective 54 while taking and planting them.</div>

      <ExpandableNote label="First-time Farming route" title="Your daily Farming circuit: trees, hops and herbs">
        <p><strong>Prepare saplings at the bank:</strong> choose unlocked seeds that are actually available, keeping the reserved Fossil Island teak seeds and quest-specific seeds aside. Put each ordinary or fruit-tree seed into a <strong>filled plant pot</strong> using a <strong>gardening trowel</strong>, water the seedling once with a watering can, then wait a few minutes for a plantable sapling. Bank unused seeds, pots and tools. Magic tree seeds require <strong>75 Farming</strong> and are <strong>not</strong> part of this 62–65 plan: retain them for later high-level Farming, contracts or optional diaries. No mandatory quest in this route consumes a magic seed; the separate <strong>13 magic logs</strong> allocation is sourced independently.</p>
        <p><strong>Prepare the fully supplied circuit before leaving</strong> (omit crops and payments you are deliberately skipping):</p>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>Tree stock:</strong> up to <strong>five ordinary-tree</strong> and <strong>four fruit-tree saplings</strong>, plus the protection payments appropriate to the species you actually plant or compost for trees you leave unprotected. A smaller mixed run is fine.</li>
          <li><strong>Hops, herbs and limpwurts:</strong> <strong>8 Yanillian seeds</strong> (four per hops planting), <strong>two useful banked herb seeds</strong>, and up to <strong>two banked limpwurt seeds</strong> (one per flower patch). Check the bank first; missing Yanillian seeds can be bought at Olivia's Draynor Seed Market or Amelia's Farming Guild seed shop when accessible. Limpwurt seeds are <em>not</em> part of those shops' reliable stock: retain seeds from drops or pickpocket a Master Farmer at 38 Thieving if you want to grow the missing roots. Do not wait for seed luck—Temple of Ikov has a direct-root fallback below.</li>
          <li><strong>Protection and compost:</strong> <strong>two full tomato baskets</strong> if protecting both hops plantings, and up to <strong>six compost applications</strong> for two hops, two herbs and two flower patches, plus any compost chosen for trees. Each full tomato basket contains five tomatoes. Hosidius flowers are disease-free after Kourend &amp; Kebos Easy; compost there is optional, while Catherby flowers retain disease risk. Gardener payment items can be noted; loose fruit and empty baskets do not replace a requested full basket.</li>
          <li><strong>Tools and travel:</strong> spade, seed dibber, rake where needed, available magic secateurs, coins for tree clearing and the Brimhaven charter, and the planned Lumbridge/Falador/Varrock/Camelot and Hosidius house teleports. Weight-reducing clothes help but are optional. Retrieve stored tools or compost from Tool Leprechauns and refresh supplies at Falador east bank, the Grand Exchange or Catherby bank as needed; do <em>not</em> try to carry every sapling, compost bucket and payment in one inventory.</li>
        </ul>
        <p>Ordinary Lumbridge/Falador/Varrock/Camelot and Teleport to House <em>spells</em> need the standard spellbook; on Ancients or Lunar, use already-made tablets where stocked, teleport jewellery/other unlocked transport plus short walks, or switch back at the proper altar before casting. No Graceful, seed box, bottomless bucket, rune pouch, Farmer's outfit, Humidify, maxed POH, timer plugin or unlimited tablet bank is assumed. Yanillian hops need <strong>16 Farming</strong>, take about <strong>60 minutes</strong> to grow and use <strong>four seeds per patch</strong>. Pay <strong>one full tomato basket per hops planting</strong> if protecting it; compost still improves the variable harvest yield. Limpwurt plants need <strong>26 Farming</strong>, take about <strong>20 minutes</strong> to grow and use <strong>one seed per flower patch</strong>; a healthy plant gives at least <strong>three roots</strong>, with variable extra yield. <strong>Magic secateurs improve limpwurt yield</strong> when equipped or carried, but the harvest still varies; count actual banked roots instead of projecting a bonus. Compost helps crop survival and is separate from the secateurs' yield effect. Keep magic secateurs equipped or in your inventory when harvesting <em>hops, herbs and limpwurts</em>, and use harvested produce on a Tool Leprechaun to note it. Paid hops need no watering or disease babysitting; herbs do not need watering and cannot be gardener-protected with tomatoes. <a href="https://secure.runescape.com/m=news/more-poll-78-changes?oldschool=1" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Jagex's secateurs and birdhouse update</a> · <a href="https://oldschool.runescape.wiki/w/Yanillian_seed" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Yanillian seed and payment details</a>. At every stop, leave crops that are still growing alone; harvest/replant instructions below apply when a patch is ready.</p>
        <ol>
          <li><strong>Lumbridge — ordinary tree + hops:</strong> Lumbridge teleport, then the tree patch west of/behind Lumbridge Castle. Run north to the hops patch north of Lumbridge farm, south-east of the Champions' Guild. Harvest if ready, compost and replant Yanillian hops while building your stock, then pay the gardener a full tomato basket if protecting them. <strong>After the hops stop, teleport to Falador.</strong></li>
          <li><strong>Falador — ordinary tree:</strong> walk to the tree in Falador Park. The <strong>east bank just south of the park</strong> can refresh supplies before you continue north-west to Taverley; a charged Ring of wealth is not required.</li>
          <li><strong>Taverley — ordinary tree:</strong> tend the Taverley patch after Falador, with no redirected tablet or house move. Then use the planned Varrock teleport.</li>
          <li><strong>Varrock — ordinary tree:</strong> tend the patch in the palace courtyard, then go to the <strong>Grand Exchange spirit tree</strong>; its bank is another resupply point.</li>
          <li><strong>Tree Gnome Stronghold — ordinary tree + fruit tree:</strong> ride the spirit tree to the Stronghold. Visit the ordinary patch south-west of its spirit tree, then the fruit patch just east of that tree. The Grand Tree and Tree Gnome Village already unlocked this network. Take the spirit tree onward to Tree Gnome Village.</li>
          <li><strong>Tree Gnome Village — fruit tree:</strong> arrive in the Village maze, ask <strong>Elkoy</strong> to escort you out, then head west/south-west to the fruit patch outside the maze. Use Camelot teleport for the next leg.</li>
          <li><strong>Seers' Village — hops:</strong> from the Camelot arrival, head north-west to the hops patch north of McGrubor's Wood. Harvest if ready, compost and replant Yanillian hops while stocking payments, and pay the gardener a full tomato basket if protecting them. Return toward Camelot, then continue east to Catherby; a second Camelot teleport is an optional walking shortcut, not a new required unlock.</li>
          <li><strong>Catherby — herbs + limpwurt flowers + fruit tree:</strong> visit the herb and flower patches at the allotments north of Catherby bank. Harvest and note herbs, compost the herb patch and replant one useful banked seed, such as plentiful harralander/tarromin. If you have a limpwurt seed and still need the Ikov stock, harvest the flower patch when ready, compost and replant one seed; bank its roots separately from the diary root already spent. Use the bank if needed, then tend the fruit-tree patch east of Catherby near the mountain/beach and continue to the docks.</li>
          <li><strong>Brimhaven — fruit tree:</strong> from Catherby docks charter to Brimhaven, then walk north to its patch. The listed fare is <strong>480 coins</strong> before discounts; the already-unlocked <strong>ring of charos(a)</strong> halves it to 240 while worn. Cabin Fever, a Brimhaven house and a planted spirit tree are not assumed. After the fruit tree, use the established Hosidius house teleport for the final stop.</li>
          <li><strong>Hosidius — herbs + limpwurt flowers:</strong> exit the Hosidius house portal and walk north toward the allotment/flower/herb patches, using the windmill as a landmark. Harvest and note herbs, compost and replant a useful seed; a scarcer desired herb makes sense here. At the adjacent flower patch, harvest any mature limpwurts and replant a banked seed while the Ikov stock is short. <strong>Kourend &amp; Kebos Easy</strong>, already complete, makes these herb and flower patches disease-free; compost still improves herb yield but is not needed to prevent flower disease. Catherby and other ordinary patches retain disease risk. No Xeric's talisman or house move is assumed.</li>
        </ol>
        <p>The fully supplied circuit covers <strong>up to fifteen patches across ten listed stops: nine trees, two hops, two herbs and two limpwurt flowers</strong>. It is a recommended route when you choose to farm, <em>not</em> a daily quota or chapter-completion condition. Skip a crop that is still growing, run fewer patches when seeds are short, and let the two herb stops continue even after the hop stock is funded. <strong>Bank and count 20 unnoted limpwurt roots before Temple of Ikov</strong>; stop replanting solely for that quest once the reserve is met. Hosidius is the one added teleport destination beyond the old tree circuit; the hops detours add walking, not a free stop. Newly planted trees have no mature health-check jackpot on the first outing. Later, at each mature tree: <strong>check health</strong> for the main Farming XP, pick useful fruit (including possible protection fruit), pay the gardener <strong>200 coins</strong> to remove the old tree, plant a replacement sapling, then pay for protection <em>or</em> compost the patch and leave. The 200-coin clearing fee is separate from the new tree's protection payment. Tree saplings do not need repeated watering once planted outdoors. A grown tree can instead be chopped for Woodcutting and its stump dug for optional roots; neither is repeatable large Farming XP from leaving the same tree there. Paying for clearing keeps the usual run short.</p>
        <div class="crop-table-wrap"><table class="crop-table"><thead><tr><th>Level</th><th>Tree / seed</th><th>Growth</th><th>Health-check XP</th><th>Gardener protection</th></tr></thead><tbody>
          <tr><td>15</td><td>Oak / acorn</td><td>3h 20m</td><td>467.3</td><td>1 basket of tomatoes</td></tr>
          <tr><td>30</td><td>Willow</td><td>~4h</td><td>1,456.5</td><td>1 basket of apples</td></tr>
          <tr><td>45</td><td>Maple</td><td>5h 20m</td><td>3,403.4</td><td>1 basket of oranges</td></tr>
          <tr><td>60</td><td>Yew</td><td>6h 40m</td><td>7,069.9</td><td>10 cactus spines</td></tr>
          <tr><td>27</td><td>Apple (fruit)</td><td>16h</td><td>1,199.5</td><td>9 sweetcorn</td></tr>
          <tr><td>33</td><td>Banana (fruit)</td><td>16h</td><td>1,750.5</td><td>4 baskets of apples</td></tr>
          <tr><td>39</td><td>Orange (fruit)</td><td>16h</td><td>2,470.2</td><td>3 baskets of strawberries</td></tr>
          <tr><td>42</td><td>Curry (fruit)</td><td>16h</td><td>2,906.9</td><td>5 baskets of bananas</td></tr>
          <tr><td>51</td><td>Pineapple (fruit)</td><td>16h</td><td>4,605.7</td><td>10 watermelons</td></tr>
          <tr><td>57</td><td>Papaya (fruit)</td><td>16h</td><td>6,146.4</td><td>10 pineapples</td></tr>
        </tbody></table></div>
        <p>These are <strong>tree health-check</strong> XP figures, excluding planting, fruit picking, hops and herbs; growth times are approximate. Use the highest useful unlocked seeds you actually have, not an imaginary daily supply. For the <strong>tree portion only</strong>, five successful maple checks plus four successful pineapple checks yield <strong>5 × 3,403.4 + 4 × 4,605.7 ≈ 35,440 Farming XP</strong> before planting/fruit XP. That assumes all nine tree seeds, relevant levels and healthy mature trees; it is <em>not</em> a guaranteed Chapter 1 daily reward. One circuit at a convenient login is enough; ordinary trees can be revisited sooner if you want, but there is no required second daily run. <a href="https://oldschool.runescape.wiki/w/Farming_training#Tree_runs" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Tree-run reference</a>.</p>
        <p><strong>Protection without a shopping marathon:</strong> <a href="https://oldschool.runescape.wiki/w/Cobado%27s_Groceries" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Cobado's Groceries</a> in Civitas illa Fortis sells cooking apples (base stock <strong>20 at 1 coin each</strong>) and tomatoes (base stock <strong>10 at 4 coins each</strong>), as well as bananas and pineapples; depleted stock changes actual prices. Buy a modest batch when already in Fortis, fill empty baskets from a Farming shop with <strong>five</strong> matching fruit each, and bank the full baskets ahead of a run. For example, five willows need <strong>five full apple baskets</strong> and four banana trees need <strong>16</strong>: <strong>21 full baskets = 105 cooking apples</strong> for that particular crop mix, gathered across shop restocks or visits, not in one purchase. One full tomato basket contains <strong>five tomatoes</strong>. Gardeners accept noted payment items, but loose apples or empty baskets do not substitute for full apple baskets. The <strong>Culinaromancer's Chest</strong> is another apple source if opened; check its live food stock, because Recipe for Disaster progress changes it and this chapter does not assume the full 50. <strong>Funch's Fine Groceries</strong> in the Grand Tree sells oranges and pineapples. Sweetcorn and watermelons come from your own allotments or existing bank, not a guaranteed shop stock. If strawberry baskets, ten watermelons, cactus spines or another payment are awkward, use compost instead and accept disease risk. For ultracompost, use <strong>two volcanic ash on one bucket of supercompost</strong> (Fossil Island ash is mineable); it greatly reduces but does not eliminate disease. It does not multiply tree fruit/health-check XP. Prioritise scarce mahogany over cheap trees when shopping is tiresome; protection is optional, not a reason to stall the route.</p>
        <p><strong>Fossil Island hardwood exception:</strong> check the health of the two <strong>western teak trees</strong> from the earlier Chapter 2 timber prep, but <strong>leave them planted</strong> while they are supplying Construction logs. Do not clear and replant them just for another Farming check. Begin banking harvested Yanillian hops before 55 Farming if you have mahogany seeds. The unreserved eastern patch can rotate separately; see the <a href="#i14-hardwood-run">optional hardwood walkthrough below</a> for patch availability, protection and the occasional run. Do not count hardwoods in this daily circuit.</p>
        <p><strong>Stop when the near-term mahogany-payment stock is ready:</strong> <strong>25 harvested hops</strong> protect one mahogany, <strong>75</strong> protect three and <strong>125</strong> protect five. Bank for the saplings you actually expect to plant. Once the reserve is funded, harvest any remaining grown hops and <strong>stop replanting</strong> until it runs low; the two herb stops can continue independently. For scale only, a bank with <strong>160 Yanillian seeds</strong> supports <strong>40 patch plantings</strong>; a plan for five mahoganies needs <strong>125 harvested hops</strong>. Neither seed count nor hop yield is a guarantee. With two patches per visit, purely illustrative averages of 10, 20 or 30 hops <em>per patch</em> would take seven, four or three visits respectively to reach 125; real harvests vary. Check the bank rather than scheduling fixed visits or hourly alarms.</p>
        <p><strong>Optional third hops patch:</strong> when already visiting Bert or the Wizards' Guild in Yanille, or if you deliberately want faster stock, use Yanille's hops patch <strong>inside the north town wall near the Wizards' Guild</strong>. After completing Watchtower, a <strong>level-58 Magic standard-spellbook Watchtower teleport</strong> lands at the Watchtower outside the north-west of Yanille; use the nearby 16-Agility north-wall shortcut or enter through the town gates, then walk to the patch. This does <em>not</em> assume Ardougne Hard's relocated city-centre teleport. Entrana and other herb patches are later/opportunistic expansions, not part of this compact default loop.</p>
      </ExpandableNote>

      <div id="i14-hardwood-run" style="scroll-margin-top: 6rem;">
        <ExpandableNote label="Optional background Farming" title="Optional hardwood run: mahoganies every few days">
          <p><strong>Use this only at actual 55+ Farming with mahogany seeds you want to plant.</strong> Mahoganies and teaks use dedicated <strong>hardwood patches</strong>; neither can be planted in the ordinary-tree or fruit-tree patches of the daily circuit. The two wild mahogany trees north of the Farming Guild are for chopping logs: you cannot plant saplings there or claim Farming health-check XP. The Guild's ordinary-tree and fruit-tree patches are not hardwood patches either. This separate, occasional trip does not change the daily circuit's <strong>nine trees + two hops + two herbs + two flowers = up to 15 patches across ten travel stops</strong>.</p>
          <p><strong>Choose the patches that are actually free.</strong> Fossil Island has <strong>three</strong> hardwood patches. This guide reserves the <strong>two western patches</strong> for the resident teaks supplying Chapter 2.1 Construction timber: check their health once if you have not already, then <strong>leave both planted while their logs are still needed</strong>. The remaining <strong>eastern patch</strong> is the default rotating mahogany. If you never planted those teaks, use any genuinely empty patch; once their timber work is finished, you may clear a former teak and rotate that patch too. Chapter 2.1's Castle Wars wild-teak fallback remains available. Never assume that all three Fossil patches should be cleared for mahoganies.</p>
          <p><strong>Optional second patch:</strong> complete <strong>The Ribbiting Tale of a Lily Pad Labour Dispute</strong> early if you want Marcellus' dedicated hardwood patch at <strong>Locus Oasis</strong>. Children of the Sun is already complete; the quest also needs <strong>actual 15 Woodcutting</strong> and an axe. Bring any axe, or take the bronze axe available during the quest. Talk to <strong>Marcellus at Locus Oasis</strong> to begin and use the <a href="https://oldschool.runescape.wiki/w/The_Ribbiting_Tale_of_a_Lily_Pad_Labour_Dispute/Quick_guide" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">current short quest guide</a> for its dialogue and puzzles. This completion and its Woodcutting XP are <em>optional now</em>, not part of guaranteed Chapter 1 floors; Chapter 3.5 still finishes the quest for players who skip it here. With the two Construction teaks standing, the unlocked Locus patch plus the eastern Fossil patch allow <strong>two</strong> rotating mahoganies. A Fossil-Island-only run is fully valid.</p>
          <p><strong>Prepare saplings at a bank:</strong> put each mahogany seed into a <strong>filled plant pot</strong> with a gardening trowel, water the seedling once, and wait a few minutes for the sapling. Bank the potting supplies afterward. For <strong>N</strong> mahoganies you actually plan to plant or replant, bring:</p>
          <ul>
            <li><strong>N mahogany saplings</strong> and, if buying gardener protection, <strong>25 × N harvested Yanillian hops</strong> (not seeds). Gardeners accept the hops <strong>noted</strong>; protection is recommended for slow, scarce trees but is not required to plant them. Two mahoganies take <strong>two saplings and 50 harvested hops</strong>.</li>
            <li>A <strong>spade</strong>, <strong>rake</strong> if a patch has weeds, and <strong>200 coins per grown tree you choose to have cleared</strong>. The clearing coins and hops are separate payments. If you skip protection, bring ordinary compost/supercompost/ultracompost if available; it reduces disease risk but cannot guarantee survival.</li>
            <li>For Fossil Island, a <strong>charged Digsite pendant</strong> with its Fossil Island destination unlocked. Only if visiting Locus Oasis, also bring the already-owned <strong>Ardougne cloak 1</strong> and a <strong>dramen staff</strong> for the fairy rings. You need an axe only for the optional quest or if you choose to chop a tree rather than pay for clearing. No quetzal whistle, landing-site construction, moved house, POH fairy ring, Graceful, seed box, bottomless bucket, rune pouch or Tithe reward is assumed.</li>
          </ul>
          <p>Keep the <strong>20-root Temple of Ikov reserve</strong> untouched: mahogany protection takes <strong>hops, not limpwurt roots</strong>. If you later choose to protect a newly planted teak, that is a different optional payment of <strong>15 surplus limpwurt roots per teak</strong>, never a reason to clear the two resident timber trees.</p>
          <ol>
            <li><strong>Locus Oasis — skip when the quest is not complete:</strong> use the Ardougne cloak's monastery teleport, walk east past the Tower of Life to fairy ring <strong>DJP</strong> with your dramen staff, choose <strong>AJP</strong>, then run south-east to Locus Oasis. Visit the dedicated hardwood patch beside <strong>Marcellus</strong> on the eastern side of the oasis, just south of the pond. Plant/protect on the first visit, or check and replace a mature rotating tree as below.</li>
            <li><strong>Fossil Island:</strong> use the Digsite pendant's <strong>Fossil Island / House on the Hill</strong> destination, take the nearby <strong>Magic Mushtree</strong> to <strong>Mushroom Meadow</strong>, then run <strong>south-east</strong> along the path to the three clustered hardwood patches in the Mushroom Forest. Identify the <strong>unreserved eastern patch before clearing anything</strong>; the two western teaks stay for Construction logs until that job is finished. Plant/protect or check/replant at the appropriate <strong>squirrel gardener</strong>. This path does <em>not</em> use the Museum Camp 70-Agility shortcut. If convenient, pair this visit with the Mushroom Meadow part of an existing birdhouse run, but birdhouses are optional and no new timer is required.</li>
          </ol>
          <p><strong>First planting:</strong> clear weeds if present, plant the sapling, pay Marcellus or the squirrel <strong>25 noted or unnoted Yanillian hops</strong> if choosing protection, then leave. <strong>On a later mature-tree visit:</strong> <strong>check health first</strong> for the Farming reward, pay the gardener <strong>200 coins</strong> to clear <em>only the rotating XP tree</em>, plant a fresh sapling and pay its new protection separately if chosen. Do not pay to remove a resident timber teak while its logs are needed. No repeated watering is needed after planting outdoors. Leaving a mature tree standing supplies logs, not another health-check reward; a fresh planting starts another XP cycle. Compost can reduce disease risk when not paying a gardener, but it does not raise the tree's health-check XP.</p>
          <p>Each healthy mahogany gives <strong>63 Farming XP when planted</strong> and <strong>15,720 XP when its health is checked</strong>: <strong>15,783 combined XP</strong> across the two visits, or <strong>31,566 combined XP</strong> for two successful trees. Growth takes about <strong>85 hours 20 minutes (3 days 13 hours 20 minutes)</strong>, so checking around every four days is convenient, not a deadline or promised date for 62 Farming. Seeds and hop harvests are variable: with no spare seed, skip this optional run; with too few hops, plant with compost and disease risk or protect only the trees you can fund. Either way, continue the main guide. <a href="https://oldschool.runescape.wiki/w/Mahogany_seed" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Mahogany growth and XP</a> · <a href="https://oldschool.runescape.wiki/w/Farming_patches" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Hardwood patch locations</a>.</p>
        </ExpandableNote>
      </div>

      <ExpandableNote label="Seed supply" title="Running low on tree seeds?">
        <ol>
          <li><strong>Search the bank first:</strong> use existing lower-tier tree/fruit saplings and seeds; search unsearched seed nests. Collect already-earned unclaimed Wintertodt rewards if any remain, but do not return for another long Wintertodt grind.</li>
          <li><strong>Birdhouses are available now:</strong> use the <a href={`${base}intermediate/chapter-1-3/#i13-birdhouses`}>Chapter 1.3 birdhouse primer</a>. Bring a Digsite pendant, hammer/chisel, suitable logs and cheap bait seeds; collect houses, reuse clockworks, reset them, then search seed nests. Do it when convenient, not on a 50-minute alarm. Maple/pineapple seeds are possible but random, never promised.</li>
          <li><strong>Try attainable Farming Contracts:</strong> at actual 45 (or 42 with a Garden pie), fairy ring CIR plus a south-west walk reaches Jane and easy contracts. Bring a few basic allotment/flower/bush seeds from the bank or seed shop, accept a crop you can actually grow, harvest/check it inside the Guild, then open the <em>random</em> seed pack. Easy contracts cannot be cancelled; medium/hard can be downgraded. Boost again before requesting another boosted tier. This is a seed source, not guaranteed tree-seed self-sufficiency.</li>
          <li><strong>Later, not now:</strong> Managing Miscellania unlocks in <strong>Chapter 2.3</strong>. Maple workers can add nests/seeds then, but consume treasury coins; do not bring the kingdom quest forward or drain herb/timber funding just to claim seed income.</li>
          <li><strong>If tree seeds run dry:</strong> run fewer ordinary patches, keep available fruit/hardwoods going, and grow useful herbs or allotments while waiting. <strong>Olivia in Draynor</strong> stocks basic allotment/hops/flower seeds; Master Farmers can supplement herbs/allotments, but neither reliably supplies the tree seeds for this circuit. No Grand Exchange, compulsory Mole, Puro-Puro, bossing or Tithe is required. A dry bank may slow passive Farming; check your live level at the later 49/70 consumers and use a bounded active bridge only if you choose or need it.</li>
        </ol>
        <p>Keep a <em>modest</em> useful contract buffer if one exists, but do not freeze your last five ordinary seeds for hypothetical future assignments. Preserve named teak/quest seeds. Banked <strong>magic seeds</strong> remain untouched here until 75 Farming; they are not substitutes for the <strong>13 magic logs</strong> reserved for Desert Treasure and Mourning's End Part I.</p>
      </ExpandableNote>

      <p><strong>Other side crops remain optional:</strong> the combined circuit above is enough to introduce useful supplies without a second full-map run. Add other herb patches later or when already passing them; no early Explorer's ring, Ardougne cloak 2, Ectophial, basalt teleport or intermediate Farming Guild wing is required. The one required whiteberry has its explicit drop fallback, while the required limpwurt-root stock is handled in the main circuit and at Ikov below. Birdhouses supply Hunter XP and possible seed nests; giant seaweed at the two underwater Fossil Island patches is mainly a future <strong>Crafting</strong> supply, not a Farming XP obligation. Visit these only when they fit your session.</p>

      <ExpandableNote label="Optional active method" title="Optional active method: Tithe Farm and targeted rewards">
        <p><strong>Choose this only for a specific XP bridge or reward.</strong> Tithe Farm is in south-east Hosidius; from the already-moved Hosidius POH, leave the house and head east. Bring a spade, seed dibber and about 8–10 filled watering cans; the nearby Tool Leprechaun can supply ordinary tools. Entry begins at <strong>34 Farming</strong>, covered by this chapter's quests. It is a timed planting/watering activity, not AFK, and needs no Humidify.</p>
        <ol>
          <li>Take the highest seeds your <em>current effective level</em> allows: Golovanova at 34, Bologano at 54 or Logavano at 74. Boosts must be live while taking/planting seeds, not throughout harvest.</li>
          <li>Plant and immediately water <strong>8–20 patches</strong>; return for <strong>two more watering passes</strong> as they advance, then harvest and deposit the fruit. A comfortable <strong>20-plant cycle is fully valid</strong>. There is no obligation to graduate to 24–25 plants or use animation skips. Mature plants no longer decay, but missing an earlier timed watering can kill them.</li>
          <li>A full <strong>100-fruit</strong> sack earns <strong>35 points</strong> and includes the large 75th-fruit XP bonus. That can be <strong>five successful 20-plant cycles</strong>, not 100 simultaneous plants. Break at a natural point and stop when the chosen XP gap or reward is met.</li>
        </ol>
        <p><strong>Spend only for a benefit you want:</strong> check existing points first. <strong>Auto-weed (50)</strong> is the smallest useful crop-run convenience; from zero points two complete 100-fruit batches cover it. Turn it on at Gricoller's reward shop, rake any weeds already present once, then ordinary patches stay clear while enabled. Plain raking works perfectly without it. <strong>Herb sack (250)</strong> is useful later for grimy-herb drops and requires <strong>actual 58 Herblore to buy/use</strong>; alternatively it costs <strong>750 Slayer reward points</strong>, which are scarce and need not be spent here. <strong>Seed box (250)</strong> holds up to six <em>seed types</em>, not saplings, and is only carrying convenience; it is not automatically more valuable than the Herb sack for someone who dislikes Tithe. From zero, either 250-point item takes about <strong>eight complete 100-fruit batches</strong>, a very different commitment from Auto-weed. Gricoller's can (200) and the Farmer's outfit (400 total) are optional investments. No reward is a required Chapter 1 or later graduation item.</p>
        <p>After any Tithe session, resume ordinary quests and the passive crop routine. If giant seaweed interests you, its two underwater Fossil Island patches require 23 Farming and seaweed spores; dive from the northern rowboat with Farming tools and compost, mind oxygen without diving gear, and collect occasional seafloor spore spawns. Growth is roughly 40 minutes and its payoff is later Crafting supplies. Neither seaweed nor a Tithe shop purchase is required progress. <a href="https://oldschool.runescape.wiki/w/Tithe_Farm" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Tithe Farm mechanics and rewards</a>.</p>
      </ExpandableNote>

      <TrainingOptions id="i14-hunter-training" skill="Hunter" target="46" why="Birdhouses should already have supplied much of this level. 46 is the Chapter 1 benchmark and leaves the account in a good place for future Hunter content." recommended="Keep doing birdhouse runs until 46 Hunter. If you are only a few levels short, there is no need to force a separate grind." relaxed="Wait for additional birdhouse cycles across normal play sessions." resource="If you want to finish immediately, use a conventional Hunter method appropriate to your level." fast="Kebbit/chinchompa routes are available if you want faster active XP." />

      <RouteStep id="i14-ikov" number="17" title="Complete Temple of Ikov without hunting a yew longbow">
        <p><strong>Check the bank before travelling:</strong> bring <strong>20 unnoted limpwurt roots</strong>, a light source, weight-reducing gear, and <strong>darts or throwing knives</strong>. Steel arrows do <em>not</em> replace the thrown ammunition in the Fire Warrior workaround. Count existing knives/darts first; if short, prepare about <strong>50 iron knives</strong> as a starter. At actual 50 Agility and Thieving from 1.2, buy them from <strong>Martin Thwait beneath the Toad and Chicken in Burthorpe's Rogues' Den</strong> (only ten stocked per world, so allow restocks or other worlds), or at 22 Smithing use a hammer and anvil to turn <strong>10 iron bars into 50 knives</strong>. This is not a 1,200-knife training grind; if combat consumes the first stack, return for only another 25–50. The Kourend diary already spent its separate root; count only the roots actually on hand now. If your flower harvests or seed drops ran short, take ordinary combat gear, food and a Hosidius house teleport to the <strong>level-28 hobgoblins on the peninsula north-east of the Hosidius Vinery</strong>. Kill them for their repeatable but random limpwurt-root drops, bank the missing roots, and stop exactly when you hold 20. This direct-root recovery needs no limpwurt seeds or extra Farming wait. Then complete <strong>Temple of Ikov</strong>.</p>
        <p>You do <strong>not</strong> need to grind Puro-Puro for a yew longbow. In the ice chamber, search the chests until you obtain at least <strong>1 ice arrow</strong>. Equip the ice arrow in your ammo slot, then use darts/throwing knives on the Fire Warrior of Lesarkus; the current quest mechanics allow thrown weapons to bypass the normal yew-bow requirement. Wear Ava's attractor and retrieve recoverable knives when safe, but top up the actual shortfall rather than assuming recovery makes the starter stack infinite.</p>
        <p><strong>Before leaving the bank:</strong> check the quest-specific quantities and sources in the preparation note.</p>
        <ExpandableNote label="Quest preparation" title="Light, knife and an actual ammunition stack">
          <p>Bring a <strong>knife</strong> as well as the lit light source to cut the web and collect the boots of lightness. Buy the knife from a general store and a candle from Catherby's candle shop, then light it with a tinderbox. Remove heavy equipment at the bridge and use the quest boots so your carried weight is negative; do not assume an unowned Graceful outfit. The knife/bow workaround still needs the quest's ice arrow equipped and a real stack of thrown ammunition. For self-made replacement knives, mine/buy iron ore and smelt until the required bars succeed; earlier quest bars are spent.</p>
        </ExpandableNote>
      </RouteStep>

      <RouteStep id="i14-dt" number="18" title="Complete Desert Treasure with a supplied, no-flick boss plan">
        <p>With <strong>The Dig Site, The Tourist Trap, Troll Stronghold, Priest in Peril, Waterfall Quest</strong> and <strong>Temple of Ikov</strong> completed, the quest prerequisites are done. Check <strong>actual 53 Thieving</strong>: 1.2 guarantees only 50. If still short after the intervening quests, use Wealthy Citizens/house valuables or Ardougne Knights for the live remainder. Also confirm <strong>50 Magic, 50 Firemaking, 10 Slayer and 43 Prayer</strong>; 43 Prayer is this guide's combat preparation, not a quest-start requirement.</p>
        <p><strong>The default is to leave the appropriate protection prayer on, bank between diamonds and use the boss-specific positioning below.</strong> One-tick flicking, alternating overheads every tick, ice gloves and super restores are not assumed. Ordinary restores solve Kamil's drained Magic; they do <em>not</em> restore Prayer. Prepare the two potion types separately before going to the ice area.</p>
        <p><strong>Opening bank check:</strong> 12 magic logs, 6 steel bars, 6 molten glass, 1 ashes, 1 charcoal, 1 blood rune, 1 ordinary bones, 1 silver bar, garlic, pestle and mortar, spice, one chocolate bar/cake, climbing boots, 1 iron bar, gas mask, tinderbox, about 50 lockpicks, Shantay passes for any desert gate crossings, waterskins for desert walks, and at least 650 coins plus travel money. These are quest-wide supplies; withdraw only the current leg's items. Open the preparation notes before the first trip.</p>
        <p><strong>Eblis departure:</strong> withdraw <strong>12 magic logs, 6 steel bars and 6 molten glass as three noted stacks</strong>. Bring ashes, charcoal, one blood rune and ordinary bones alongside them. These materials use <strong>seven inventory slots</strong>, leaving room for coins, desert travel supplies, food and a teleport. Keep the <strong>thirteenth magic log banked for Mourning's End Part I</strong>. The glass must still be <strong>molten glass</strong>; noting it does not mean crafting it into another item.</p>

        <ExpandableNote label="Quest preparation" title="Where every non-combat item comes from">
          <ul>
            <li><strong>12 magic logs:</strong> use the protected Woodcutting/Wintertodt stock and withdraw the twelve <strong>noted for Eblis</strong>. If fewer remain, cut the missing logs at 75 Woodcutting; return to the opening Woodcutting step for that conditional gate. This quest spends twelve of the chapter's thirteen-log allocation; keep the last log banked for Mourning's End Part I. Chapter 3.1's While Guthix Sleeps supplies the later MM2 balloon logs from its storeroom loot.</li>
            <li><strong>6 steel bars + 1 iron bar:</strong> check the bank after clockworks, other quests and smithing. At 30 Smithing, smelt six iron ore with twelve coal into steel at an ordinary furnace; make the separate iron bar at 15 Smithing, allowing for failed iron smelts. Mine the ore or buy it from Ordan in Keldagrim. Six iron ore alone is not six steel bars. Withdraw the <strong>six steel bars noted for Eblis</strong>; keep the separate iron bar for the boots stage.</li>
            <li><strong>6 molten glass:</strong> withdraw the reserved glass as <strong>one noted stack of six molten glass for Eblis</strong>. It must remain molten glass. If it was blown into something else, replace it with six fresh buckets of sand and six soda ash from a charter trader, smelted at a furnace at 1 Crafting. Unpowered orbs cannot be converted back into glass.</li>
            <li><strong>1 silver bar:</strong> a fresh bar after Shadow of the Storm, Nature Spirit, Fenkenstrain and Animal Magnetism. Buy the shortfall from the East Ardougne Silver Merchant; do not assume one reserved bar survived all five consumers.</li>
            <li><strong>Ashes / bones / blood rune:</strong> burn a normal log and collect its ashes; kill a chicken for ordinary bones; if already at 63 Magic, drink a Wizard's mind bomb to enter the 66-Magic Wizards' Guild in Yanille and buy a blood rune upstairs; otherwise buy it from Ali Morrisane after The Feud and Rogue Trader's rune-shop puzzle, or Lundail's shop using 1.3's bank-first Wilderness approach. A rune shop without blood-rune stock is not a supplier.</li>
            <li><strong>Charcoal / garlic powder / spice / sweet:</strong> buy charcoal from Jiminua north-west of Tai Bwo Wannai, take garlic from the cupboard upstairs in Morgan's Draynor house and grind it, buy spice from Ardougne's spice seller, and buy a chocolate bar from the Grand Tree groceries. Keep that sweet separate from chocolate dust used in energy potions.</li>
            <li><strong>Boots:</strong> take one spare climbing-boot pair from Death Plateau, or buy it from Tenzing for 12 coins. Bring it and the separate iron bar to <strong>Dunstan in Burthorpe</strong> for spiked boots when the ice-path stage calls for them. Keep an ordinary climbing pair available for mountain travel.</li>
            <li><strong>Desert entry:</strong> Shantay at the pass south of Al Kharid sells passes and filled waterskins. Buy passes for the gate crossings you actually plan to make, or use an existing desert-entry bypass; carry waterskins for exposed walks. Keep carpet fares separate from the <strong>650-coin</strong> Bandit Camp pub payment, and refill travel supplies before another desert trip.</li>
            <li><strong>Gas mask / tinderbox / lockpicks:</strong> use Plague City's mask; reclaim it from the cupboard in Edmond's house if lost. Buy a tinderbox from a general store. At the route's 50+ Agility/Thieving, buy lockpicks from <strong>Martin Thwait in the Rogues' Den beneath Burthorpe's Toad and Chicken</strong>. Start with about 50 and replenish if unlucky; this is a buffer, not a guaranteed lock count. Bring antipoison and food for the trapped chest.</li>
          </ul>
          <p>The archaeologist/Rasolo and other quest NPCs supply the etchings, translation, visibility ring, keys and diamonds during their respective stages. For Dessous, <strong>first ask Malak in the Canifis pub how to kill him</strong>; Ruantun will not make the pot before that dialogue. Then take the silver bar to <strong>Ruantun in Draynor Sewers</strong> for the <strong>silver pot</strong>, bank weapons/armour for Entrana and have its High Priest bless it, return to Malak for blood, and add the garlic powder and ordinary spice. Check that the finished pot contains blood, garlic and spices before going to the graveyard. Bank completed diamonds between trips. Keep the gas mask <strong>equipped throughout the Smoke Dungeon</strong>.</p>
        </ExpandableNote>

        <div id="i14-potion-supplies">
          <ExpandableNote label="Quest preparation" title="Prayer, restore, energy and antipoison: recipes, secondaries and live quantities">
            <p><strong>Count doses and missing ingredients now.</strong> A normal Herblore craft makes <strong>three doses</strong>. Decant by using matching potions on one another, or ask Bob Barter at the Grand Exchange to decant to four-dose vials; decanting changes bottles, not the total doses. Water-filled vials are sold individually or in packs by Aemad in East Ardougne and Herblore shops.</p>
            <ul>
              <li><strong>Prayer potion — 38 Herblore:</strong> clean ranarr + vial of water + snape grass. Twelve ranarrs with only two snape grass currently make <strong>2 prayer(3), or 6 doses</strong>. Pick <strong>10 more snape grass</strong> on the hobgoblin peninsula <strong>west of the Crafting Guild</strong>, outside the guild; bring food and a Falador/bank teleport. With twelve total grass and water-filled vials, twelve crafts make <strong>36 doses = 9 prayer(4)</strong>. Farming snape grass is not required. Reserve Eadgar's separate ranarr first if that quest is unfinished.</li>
              <li><strong>Ordinary restore — 22 Herblore:</strong> clean harralander + vial of water + <strong>red spiders' eggs</strong>. For the separate troll and Kamil trips, start by making <strong>6 restore(3) = 18 doses</strong>, requiring <strong>6 harralanders and 6 eggs</strong>. Reserve <strong>12 doses = 3 restore(4)</strong> for the Kamil boss trip; the other six doses are the opening troll-trip pool. After decanting the full stock, that is four restore(4) plus one restore(2). Refill anything consumed before taking the full boss reserve. These restore drained combat stats including Magic, but <strong>not Prayer, Hitpoints or Agility</strong>. Keep the additional Heroes' Quest harralander banked.</li>
              <li><strong>Missing eggs:</strong> enter <strong>Varrock Sewers through the manhole east of Varrock Palace</strong>, follow the sewer west to the moss-giant/deadly-red-spider end, and pick the red-spiders'-eggs ground spawns. Bring a knife or slash weapon for webs, food and a bank teleport. Pick up respawns until you have the six missing eggs; killing the spiders is unnecessary. At <strong>51 Agility</strong>, the pipe near Vannaka connects Edgeville Dungeon to these sewers as an alternative. Stay out of Edgeville's separate Wilderness section.</li>
              <li><strong>Energy potion — 26 Herblore:</strong> clean harralander + water-filled vial + chocolate dust. Use already-made energy potions first; otherwise buy chocolate bars from Heckel Funch in the Grand Tree and crush them with a pestle and mortar. Each new energy potion needs an <strong>additional</strong> herb after the six restore herbs and Heroes' reserve. For an opening travel/kiting buffer from zero, make three energy(3) from three additional herbs and three chocolate dust: decant eight doses into two energy(4), leaving one spare dose. Walking while run energy regenerates and banking between legs reduces demand; replace only what you use.</li>
              <li><strong>Antipoison — 5 Herblore:</strong> buy ordinary antipoison(3) directly from <strong>Jiminua's Jungle Store</strong>, or mix clean marrentill + water-filled vial + unicorn horn dust. If making it, kill a unicorn south of Varrock and grind its horn. Start with two three-dose bottles in the bank, taking one for the trapped chest/pyramid legs and replacing it if spent.</li>
              <li><strong>If herbs are missing:</strong> use banked clean/grimy herbs and suitable already-planted herbs first. For an immediate repeatable source, take combat gear, food and a Falador/bank teleport to <strong>Taverley Dungeon's chaos druids</strong> near the entrance, kill them for herb drops and bank until the <strong>actual named shortfall</strong> is filled. This is a random drop hunt, not a promised number of kills. Do not process every harralander into energy potions before reserving the restore ingredients. No Farming-contract reward is treated as guaranteed.</li>
            </ul>
            <p><strong>Super restores are different:</strong> they need <strong>63 Herblore, snapdragon and red spiders' eggs</strong> and restore Prayer as well as drained stats. At 41 Herblore, use ordinary restores and prayer potions as separate supplies; there is no 63-Herblore detour for this quest.</p>
            <p><strong>How much to bring:</strong> bank the full available prayer stock, then take the per-boss amounts below. With the twelve-ranarr example, keep <strong>four prayer(4)</strong> earmarked for the ice-troll/Kamil attempt and use the other five as a starting pool for the remaining legs. Amounts depend on damage, prayer bonus, mistakes and retries; if the bank falls below the next leg's buffer, replenish it before entering. Do not try to stretch two newly-made three-dose potions over the whole quest by learning flicking under pressure.</p>
            <p><strong>What BRUHsailer does:</strong> its Chapter 1.4 Edgeville/Varrock-sewers stop collects about <strong>seven eggs</strong>, shared between diary uses, DT restores and Guthix balance. It later explicitly spends <strong>prayer potions and death runes</strong> on DT, alongside high-attention boss methods. Seven earlier eggs are not a guaranteed six-egg balance after those other consumers; this local pickup remains available if that route stop was skipped. <a href="https://github.com/umkyzn/BRUHsailer/blob/main/data/guide_data.json" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">BRUHsailer source</a>.</p>
          </ExpandableNote>
        </div>

        <ExpandableNote label="Combat preparation" title="Runes, food and a bankable starting kit">
          <p>Use the <strong>standard spellbook</strong> for the four bosses. Buy only missing air/water/fire/mind/chaos/death runes from Aubury in Varrock or Betty in Port Sarim; refill coins with the established Thieving method if needed. Buy elemental staffs from Zaff in Varrock. The MTA rune purchases are already partly spent, and the <strong>100-chaos-rune Chapter 2 reserve</strong> must remain after DT.</p>
          <p><strong>Opening spell stock, not a fixed kill guarantee:</strong> prepare roughly <strong>200 casts per boss</strong>, then replace any real shortfall before a retry. Fire Bolt needs <strong>1 chaos + 3 air + 4 fire</strong> per cast (35 Magic); a fire staff covers fire runes. Water Bolt needs <strong>1 chaos + 2 air + 2 water</strong> (23 Magic). For the no-gloves Fareed method, carry all three rune types even if you start with a staff. Higher water spells are an option if you have the level/runes, but do not make the plan depend on a staff staying equipped. Fire Bolt is deliberately low enough to survive some Kamil stat drain.</p>
          <p>Wear ordinary Magic gear already obtained: elemental staff, wizard hat/robes and an amulet of magic, with available defensive pieces that do not ruin spell accuracy. <strong>Chaos gauntlets from the completed Family Crest</strong> improve bolt spells if you select that enchantment; normal family/goldsmith/cooking gauntlets do not give the bolt bonus. Existing gear is sufficient preparation; there is no required Mystic, Barrows, Graceful or stamina grind.</p>
          <p>For boss trips, use <strong>cooked swordfish or better banked food</strong>. If the food bank is empty, harpoon swordfish/tuna at Catherby at 50 Fishing, cook them at your 60 Cooking and keep going until the next inventory is filled; buy the harpoon from Harry. Take roughly <strong>12–16 food</strong> after the current leg's potions/runes/quest items, plus a charged bank teleport. Kamil needs an item teleport such as a ring of dueling in the inventory, since drained Magic can disable teleport spells. Bank between bosses and refill Prayer at an altar before each trip.</p>
        </ExpandableNote>

        <ExpandableNote label="Boss plan" title="Fareed: water spells, the glove tooltip and optional kiting">
          <p><strong>Quest Helper's wording is about your weapon hand.</strong> Without ice gloves, Fareed can unequip your weapon when he reaches you. That includes a water staff: having water runes supplied by the staff does not protect the staff. The direct no-gloves plan is to <strong>enter with the weapon slot empty and manually cast water spells with all their air, water and catalytic runes in inventory</strong>. Bank the staff for this method; if you choose to wield one initially, leave a free inventory slot for it when disarmed. Water spells are the damaging Magic option here; gloves do not make Iban Blast or a fire spell work.</p>
          <p>Keep the gas mask on. Take <strong>1–2 prayer(4)</strong>, food and water-spell runes; leave <strong>Protect from Melee</strong> on when he can reach you and eat through occasional other damage. For the lower-damage alternative, bring energy potions, cast once and run around the room to keep distance. If he catches you, the three inventory rune stacks still let you keep manual casting. Banking/retrying is preferable to running out of water runes mid-fight.</p>
          <p><strong>Optional glove route:</strong> at 50 Mining, take a pickaxe, food, combat supplies and a teleport through White Wolf Mountain to the Ice Queen's lair, defeat her and collect <strong>ice gloves</strong>. Starting Heroes' Quest is not required. They let you keep a staff equipped for water-spell autocast. <strong>Smiths gloves (i)</strong> are the Giants' Foundry gloves combined with ice gloves; plain smiths gloves and goldsmith gauntlets do not substitute. There is no reason to grind the Foundry outfit just for Fareed. <a href="https://oldschool.runescape.wiki/w/Ice_Queen" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Ice Queen route</a>.</p>
        </ExpandableNote>

        <ExpandableNote label="Boss plan" title="Damis: establish the rat or bat block, then cast">
          <p>Take the ring of visibility, runes, food, a bank teleport and <strong>2 prayer(4)</strong> as a setup/recovery buffer. Damis's second form drains Prayer rapidly when it reaches you; a larger potion stack alone is an awkward answer to that mechanic.</p>
          <p><strong>Use the rat/bat block:</strong> keep a rat or giant bat alive in the arena, turn auto-retaliate off, and lure it between you and Damis so he cannot reach melee range. Set up while the first form is alive; use a weak hit only if needed to get the blocker following you. Leave Protect from Melee on during the lure. Once the blocker is actually holding Damis and you are taking no attacks, cast from that position and turn prayer off. Keep the block for the second form; do not run so far away that the encounter resets.</p>
          <p>If the blocker moves or dies, put Protect from Melee back on and re-establish it or teleport. The technique saves supplies through positioning; it does not require flicking. Use the current <a href="https://oldschool.runescape.wiki/w/Desert_Treasure_I#Damis_(Shadow_diamond)" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Damis positioning illustration</a> for the exact layout instead of guessing a tile from a textual direction.</p>
        </ExpandableNote>

        <ExpandableNote label="Boss plan" title="Dessous: keep one overhead on and budget food">
          <p>Bring the completed blessed blood mixture, combat runes, <strong>2 prayer(4)</strong>, plenty of food and a teleport. The relaxed default is to <strong>stand in melee range and leave Protect from Melee on</strong> while using Magic and eating through the other attack. Do not kite around the graveyard fence as though it were a permanent safespot: repeated Dessous teleports can end the encounter and cost another blood mixture.</p>
          <p>BRUHsailer's alternating Melee/Missiles prayer trick is an optional high-attention method, separate from basic lazy flicking. It is not a requirement for this route. If you leave or fail the fight, keep the blessed pot, return to Malak for fresh blood and rebuild the garlic/spice mixture before another attempt; having the old empty pot is not the same as having a usable summon mixture.</p>
        </ExpandableNote>

        <ExpandableNote label="Boss plan" title="Kamil: separate troll trip, Fire Bolt and ordinary restores">
          <ol>
            <li><strong>Clear the five ice trolls first.</strong> Bring the child's sweet, climbing boots, food, prayer potions, <strong>Fire Bolt runes and the six-dose ordinary-restore pool</strong>. The cold drains stats on this trip too; use restores to keep Fire Bolt available. The five kills persist, so leave and bank after opening the way. Refill anything used until you again have the full <strong>4 prayer(4) + 3 restore(4)</strong> boss inventory below, fresh food, full Prayer and restored stats. If more than the opening restore pool was needed for trolls, make the additional potions before returning.</li>
            <li><strong>For the boss trip:</strong> take about <strong>4 prayer(4), 3 ordinary restore(4)</strong>, Fire Bolt runes, a fire staff, food, spiked boots for the path afterwards and an item-based emergency teleport. Two rings of recoil are useful extra damage: make two sapphire rings from two additional gold bars/sapphires and enchant each with 1 cosmic + 1 water rune at 7 Magic; equip one and carry the spare. They are separate from teleport-jewellery reserves, and the fight does not depend on owning them.</li>
            <li><strong>Get him into melee range and leave Protect from Melee on.</strong> On the approach, Protect from Magic can cover the initial magic hits; once close, switch to Melee. That is one situational switch, not continuous flicking. Keep casting <strong>Fire Bolt</strong>. His freezes can interrupt attacking, so re-click the spell/boss or resume autocast when control returns.</li>
            <li><strong>Watch current Magic and Prayer separately.</strong> Drink an ordinary restore before Magic falls below Fire Bolt's <strong>35</strong> requirement; drink a prayer dose to maintain the overhead. At 63 Magic there is useful headroom, but cold keeps draining stats. Eat early enough to survive the next hit. If the first attempt is consuming the supplies faster than expected, teleport while you still can, replenish and retry.</li>
            <li><strong>After the kill:</strong> collect the dropped food and <strong>super restore</strong>, then continue the ice path wearing spiked boots. The dropped super restore can restore the Agility that ordinary restores could not; it is a post-kill aid and cannot fund the fight that earns it. Keep food for falls and the remaining cold damage, and retain fire-spell runes to break the ice around both parents at the end.</li>
          </ol>
          <p>The old rock/player-block Kamil methods are not the default because their reliability depends on positioning and changed encounter behaviour. Use the direct fire-spell plan and leave any flick practice for an easier enemy.</p>
        </ExpandableNote>

        <ExpandableNote label="Optional practice" title="Learn lazy flicking separately with RuneLite timing aids">
          <p>RuneLite's built-in <strong>Prayer</strong> plugin can show the prayer-flick indicator on the prayer orb and the prayer-dose indicator. Its built-in <strong>Metronome</strong> gives an audible game-tick beat; start with one tick per beat at a comfortable volume. These are timing aids, not automatic prayer toggles, and the beat alone does not identify an enemy's attack phase.</p>
          <p>Practise on one weak melee enemy near an altar with auto-retaliate off: watch several attacks, turn Protect from Melee on <strong>before the next attack begins</strong>, leave it through that attack, then switch off during the gap. Do not wait for the damage number. If you lose the rhythm, leave prayer on. This lazy-flick practice is much easier to learn than rapid off/on clicks every 0.6-second tick, and you can finish DT before mastering either. <a href="https://github.com/runelite/runelite/wiki/Prayer" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Prayer plugin</a> · <a href="https://github.com/runelite/runelite/wiki/Metronome" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Metronome</a>.</p>
        </ExpandableNote>

        <p><strong>Final pyramid:</strong> rebank the combat kit, bring all four diamonds, food, antipoison, some energy potions and a teleport, then place the diamonds and finish the pyramid. Traps can throw you back out; replenish used supplies before another run. Speak to Azzanadra for completion, then follow the Paddewwa step.</p>
        <div class="later-note"><strong>Optional future pickup:</strong> bank one Trollweiss for Troll Romance if your ice-area route makes it convenient. Otherwise collect it with that quest's own setup later.</div>
      </RouteStep>

      <RouteStep id="i14-paddewwa" number="19" title="Make all 108 Paddewwa teleport tablets now">
        <p>After Desert Treasure, switch to Ancient Magicks and prepare:</p>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>108 soft clay</strong></li>
          <li><strong>108 air runes</strong></li>
          <li><strong>216 law runes</strong></li>
          <li><strong>Fire staff</strong></li>
        </ul>
        <p>Check existing soft clay first. The eight bracelets of clay banked earlier each convert <strong>28 ordinary clay</strong> to soft clay while mining; from zero, four fresh bracelets cover 112 clay. Mine only the shortfall at an ordinary clay mine such as south-west Varrock, and stop when you have 108 soft clay. Check the law-rune balance <em>after</em> Mage Training Arena and earlier teleports; buy only the shortfall at a stocked rune shop, using the repeatable Thieving cash method if necessary. Travel back to the <strong>Ancient Pyramid</strong>: its <strong>ancient lectern beside the altar on the fourth level</strong> makes the tablets while Ancient Magicks is active. This is not a POH furniture requirement. At 54+ Magic, make <strong>108 Paddewwa teleport tablets</strong>. The Earth-orb grind itself uses only part of this stack, but making the full batch now avoids repeating the spellbook/lectern setup later.</p>
      </RouteStep>

      <TrainingOptions id="i14-construction-training" skill="Construction" target="39" why="Scrambled! has a hard 38 Construction requirement, while the Chapter 1 / early Chapter 2 handoff is cleaner at 39. Getting Ahead and Wintertodt repairs help, but neither guarantees the level, so finish it explicitly here." recommended="Use Mahogany Homes with your banked oak planks until 39. This is the preferred lower-intensity route." relaxed="Break the contracts into short sessions around birdhouse runs or Farming timers." resource="Use existing planks first and buy only what you need to close the gap." fast="Oak larders/furniture in your POH are faster if you want the level immediately." />

      <RouteStep id="i14-varlamore" number="20" title="Finish the Varlamore unlocks and buy the cosmic-rune total">
        <p><strong>Children of the Sun was completed in 1.2</strong>, so the Varlamore chain is ready. Complete <strong>Twilight’s Promise</strong>, then—with <strong>39 Construction</strong> now guaranteed—complete <strong>Scrambled!</strong>. Then use the Tal Teklan rune shop and other existing stock to bring your bank to <strong>4,900 cosmic runes total</strong>.</p>
        <p>The orb batch consumes <strong>4,770 cosmic runes</strong>—three per orb—so 4,900 leaves a small buffer for enchanting and mistakes. The 1.2 coin target is an opening reserve, <em>not</em> a guaranteed balance after MTA, sand, Magic training and quest purchases. Check liquid coins before buying: if short, do a bounded Wealthy Citizen/house-valuables refill in Civitas illa Fortis and return for the remaining runes; do not count banked alchables as spendable coins.</p>
        <p><strong>Before leaving the bank:</strong> check the quest-specific quantities and sources in the preparation note.</p>
        <ExpandableNote label="Quest preparation" title="Twilight's two combat styles and Scrambled's local materials">
          <p><strong>Twilight's Promise:</strong> take weapons/ammunition for <strong>two different combat styles</strong>, such as the rune sword and Magic staff/runes, plus food and a teleport. One melee weapon with different attack settings is not two styles. The quest's insignia and related objects are supplied during the quest.</p>
          <p><strong>Scrambled!:</strong> its bowl of water, <strong>2 normal planks, 6 nails, saw and hammer</strong> can all be collected during the quest's local tasks; check the nearby marked containers before shopping. Bring combat gear/food and actual 38 Construction (the preceding live check targets 39). Keep spare nails if collected because bent nails do not count as completed repairs. These are ordinary planks, not oak planks.</p>
        </ExpandableNote>
      </RouteStep>

      <RouteStep id="i14-orbs" number="21" title="Charge all 1,590 Earth orbs with the simple route">
        <p><strong>Magic and spellbook checkpoint:</strong> read your actual Magic XP after MTA, quests and jewellery. Charge Earth Orb needs <strong>60 Magic on the standard spellbook</strong>, and early 2.1 needs <strong>actual 63 Magic</strong> to boost into the Wizards' Guild. If below 63, train only the live remainder with ordinary elemental combat on safe, low-defence targets or sensible banked High Alchemy, buying runes in small batches; do not spend the nature runes intended for the 2.1 battlestaves without budgeting replacements. <strong>Switch back from Ancient Magicks to the standard spellbook at the Desert Pyramid before the first orb trip.</strong> Paddewwa tablets remain usable as teleports after switching.</p>
        <p>Use the low-complexity version rather than the gravestone/corpse method:</p>
        <ol>
          <li>Equip an <strong>earth staff</strong> and a bank teleport such as a ring of dueling.</li>
          <li>Withdraw <strong>25 unpowered orbs</strong>, <strong>75 cosmic runes</strong> and <strong>1 Paddewwa tablet</strong>.</li>
          <li>Break the Paddewwa tablet and run through Edgeville Dungeon to the <strong>Earth Obelisk</strong>.</li>
          <li>Cast Charge Earth Orb until all 25 are charged.</li>
          <li>Teleport to a bank, deposit the Earth orbs, restock, repeat.</li>
        </ol>
        <p>Continue until all <strong>1,590</strong> are charged. This is slower than an optimized death/gravestone storage route, but it is much easier to follow and produces the same Chapter 2 resource.</p>
      </RouteStep>

      <RouteStep id="i14-crafting-handoff" number="22" title="After quest XP, secure the functional Crafting-58 battlestaff start">
        <p><strong>Quest XP first → live XP check second → material grind or temporary boost last.</strong> Animal Magnetism (+1,000 Crafting), Enlightened Journey (+2,000), and The Hand in the Sand (+9,000) are now complete; finish/check the 1,590 unpowered orbs and the full jewellery stock before deciding. Read actual Crafting XP, not an old projected level. Preserve the 1,590 charged Earth orbs and every named quest resource.</p>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>Already actual 58+:</strong> you can attach Earth orbs to battlestaves in 2.1 without any pie. Do not hunt Sulliuscep caps.</li>
          <li><strong>Below 58, with caps/pies:</strong> mushroom pie gives +4 Crafting per bite. If five caps arrived during Woodcutting, make four pies (and keep one cap spare): mix four flour with water, put four pastry dough in four pie dishes, add one cap to each and bake at a range with your 60 Cooking. Bank the pies for 2.1. If you have fewer, use only a stock you judge sufficient to bridge the live XP gap; otherwise use the permanent-level option.</li>
          <li><strong>Below 58, without enough pies:</strong> train <em>only the live XP remainder</em> to actual 58 with banked gems or separately gathered sand/soda ash/glass. Do not take from the protected orb or quest supplies. Once the skill tab reads 58, stop.</li>
        </ul>
        <p><strong>This checkbox is only the Crafting-readiness outcome:</strong> actual 58 Crafting or sufficient mushroom-pie boosts to begin Earth battlestaves. Earlier steps already track actual 70 Woodcutting and 1,590 charged Earth orbs; do not count either again here. Caps and extra-glass training are methods, not separate required progress.</p>
      </RouteStep>

      <section class="completion-panel">
        <div class="section-label">Chapter 2 handoff check</div>
        <h3>Do not leave Chapter 1 with an invisible dependency missing.</h3>
        <p>Before moving into the Intermediate Chapter 2 route, confirm the following bank/state. These are the Chapter 1 resources that become relevant early enough in Chapter 2 that collecting them here is worthwhile:</p>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>1,590 charged Earth orbs</strong></li>
          <li><strong>Actual 58 Crafting or sufficient banked mushroom-pie boosts</strong> for the 2.1 Earth-battlestaff start; if naturally 58+, no pies or caps are required</li>
          <li><strong>2,000 stardust</strong>, unspent, for the Celestial ring</li>
          <li><strong>At least 100 chaos runes</strong> still banked; the green-dragon step deliberately swaps to Water Strike instead of consuming this reserve</li>
          <li><strong>At least 1 spare molten glass</strong>; the 1,600-glass batch leaves two initially after 1,590 orbs + 6 Desert Treasure glass + 1 Hand in the Sand lens + 1 crafting-table glass. If Pirate Pete used one for a fishbowl, keep the other for early Chapter 2</li>
          <li><strong>Only if obtained:</strong> a regular gout tuber for a future optional Karamja Medium Diary; it has no Chapter 2 mandatory consumer</li>
          <li><strong>1 harralander</strong> reserved for Heroes' Quest</li>
          <li><strong>1 bucket of sap</strong> reserved for Icthlarin's Little Helper</li>
          <li><strong>10 buckets of slime</strong> from 1.2; early Chapter 2 uses most of them around the Ectofuntus/Ghosts Ahoy sequence</li>
          <li><strong>15 salmon</strong> reserved from the Fishing section for the later Myreque quest chain</li>
          <li><strong>Heavy fishing rod</strong> kept from Barbarian Training; Chapter 2 returns to Barbarian Fishing</li>
          <li><strong>2 teak trees planted/growing</strong> in the western Fossil Island hardwood patches if seed RNG allowed it</li>
          <li><strong>Ava’s attractor</strong> unlocked from Animal Magnetism and <strong>at least 75 steel arrows</strong> kept available for the accumulator upgrade at 50 Ranged</li>
          <li><strong>Kharedst's memoirs</strong>, <strong>crystal saw</strong>, <strong>gas mask</strong>, <strong>rune sword</strong>, green d'hide chaps/vambraces and your teleport jewelry</li>
          <li><strong>2 spare goutweed</strong> banked for much later quest use</li>
        </ul>
        <p><strong>Dragon Slayer I was started only to obtain and equip the anti-dragon shield</strong> for the Prayer block; it is finished coherently in 2.2. Monkey Madness I, Black Knights' Fortress, Death to the Dorgeshuun, Underground Pass and Alfred Grimhand's Barcrawl do not need to be partially started.</p>
      </section>

      <section id="chapter-complete" class="completion-panel">
        <div class="section-label">Chapter 1 complete</div>
        <h3>The account now has a midgame backbone.</h3>
        <p>You now have passive Hunter, fairy rings, a <strong>Farming tree/fruit-tree routine</strong> with easy contracts when eligible, protection prayers, Ava’s attractor, Ancient Magicks, a practical teleport network, the exact early-Chapter-2 carryovers above, and <strong>1,590 Earth orbs</strong> ready for the battlestaff/alchemy money loop. Farming may still be below 62; keep checking live levels at the later quest gates.</p>
      </section>

